OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026", "title": "Squarefree values of quartics and power-free values of polynomials", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/manuscript.pdf", "family": "020", "family_title": "Squarefree quartics and power-free polynomial values", "abstract_html": "We prove that every irreducible integer quartic with no fixed prime-square divisor takes squarefree values with the predicted positive Euler-product density. More generally, we obtain the corresponding \\((d-2)\\)-power-free density in degrees \\(4\\le d\\le8\\). The proof combines number-field factorization, determinant estimates with adaptive auxiliary primes, and explicit low-degree geometry. Together with Browning's theorem for higher degrees, this gives the \\((d-2)\\)-power-free density for every <i>d</i> ≥ 4.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For an integer \\(k\\ge 2\\), an integer \\(a\\) is \\(k\\)-power-free if no prime power \\(p^k\\) divides \\(a\\).", "statement": "<p>For an integer \\(k\\ge 2\\), an integer \\(a\\) is \\(k\\)-power-free if no prime power \\(p^k\\) divides \\(a\\). This convention includes negative integers and excludes zero. For \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\), define\n\\[\n\\rho_f(q)=\\#\\{a\\in\\mathbb Z/q\\mathbb Z:f(a)\\equiv0\\pmod q\\},\n\\qquad\nS_{f,k}(X)=\\#\\{1\\le n\\le X:f(n)\\text{ is }k\\text{-power-free}\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be irreducible over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), of degree \\(d\\) with \\(4\\le d\\le8\\), and put \\(k=d-2\\). Assume\n\\[\n\\rho_f(p^k)&lt;p^k\\qquad\\text{for every prime }p. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nS_{f,k}(X)=c_{f,k}X+o_f(X),\n\\qquad\nc_{f,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^k)}{p^k}\\right)&gt;0.\n\\]\nNo primitivity or sign condition on the leading coefficient is required. The error term is not asserted to be uniform as \\(f\\) varies.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(f=scg\\) with primitive positive-leading \\(g\\). It has the same degree \\(4\\le d\\le8\\), and Proposition 11 gives the large-prime tail estimate for \\(g\\), without requiring local admissibility. Proposition 1 then retains the exact finite-prime conditions of the original \\(f\\), including content primes, and gives\n\\[\nS_{f,d-2}(X)=\nX\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^{d-2})}{p^{d-2}}\\right)+o_f(X).\n\\]\nThe product is positive by local admissibility and Proposition 1's convergence argument.</p><p>In particular, the theorem applies to \\(x^4+2\\) and \\(x^4+1\\). The first is Eisenstein at \\(2\\); its values at \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) exclude respectively a fixed odd prime square and a fixed square of \\(2\\). The second is the eighth cyclotomic polynomial, and its value at zero is \\(1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be irreducible over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), of degree \\(d\\ge4\\), and put \\(k=d-2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be irreducible over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), of degree \\(d\\ge4\\), and put \\(k=d-2\\). Define \\(\\rho_f\\) and \\(S_{f,k}\\) as in Theorem 1. Assume that \\(f\\) has no fixed prime \\(k\\)-th-power divisor, equivalently\n\\[\n\\rho_f(p^k)&lt;p^k\\qquad\\text{for every prime }p.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nS_{f,k}(X)=c_{f,k}X+o_f(X),\n\\qquad\nc_{f,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^k)}{p^k}\\right)&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(4\\le d\\le8\\), apply Theorem 1. For \\(d\\ge9\\),\n\\[\nd-2\\ge(3d+1)/4.\n\\]\nBrowning's theorem, in the formulation and published reproof cited as Xiao [27, Theorem 9.1 and Section 9], therefore applies to the primitive positive-leading part of \\(f\\). Its count uses positive inputs and the Euler product over all residue classes modulo \\(p^k\\). Proposition 1's sign-and-content transfer retains the original local factors and proves the conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "separable products and simultaneous values", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix an integer \\(k\\ge2\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix an integer \\(k\\ge2\\). A nonzero polynomial is called separable over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) here if each of its nonconstant irreducible factors occurs with multiplicity one.</p><p>First, let \\(F\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) be separable over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), with every nonconstant irreducible factor of degree at most \\(k+2\\). If\n\\[\n\\rho_F(p^k)&lt;p^k\\qquad\\text{for every prime }p,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nS_{F,k}(N)=c_{F,k}N+o_{F,k}(N),\n\\qquad\nc_{F,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_F(p^k)}{p^k}\\right)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>More generally, let \\(\\mathbf F=(F_1,\\ldots,F_r)\\), with \\(r\\ge1\\), be a fixed family of nonzero integer polynomials, each separable over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), whose nonconstant irreducible factors have degree at most \\(k+2\\). Factors may be shared by different \\(F_i\\). Put\n\\[\n\\Omega_p=\\{a\\in\\mathbb Z/p^k\\mathbb Z:p^k\\mid F_i(a)\\text{ for at least one }i\\},\n\\]\nand let \\(S_{\\mathbf F,k}(N)\\) count the integers \\(1\\le n\\le N\\) for which every \\(F_i(n)\\) is \\(k\\)-power-free. If\n\\[\n\\#\\Omega_p&lt;p^k\\qquad\\text{for every prime }p,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nS_{\\mathbf F,k}(N)=c_{\\mathbf F,k}N+o_{\\mathbf F,k}(N),\n\\qquad\nc_{\\mathbf F,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\#\\Omega_p}{p^k}\\right)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The first assertion requires the entire product value \\(F(n)\\) to be \\(k\\)-power-free. The second imposes power-freeness separately on the \\(F_i(n)\\), not on their product. All polynomials, their number, and \\(k\\) are fixed; no uniform or power-saving error is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Factor\n\\[\nF_i=c_i\\prod_{g\\in\\mathcal G_i}g,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), and \\(\\mathcal G_i\\) consists of distinct primitive irreducible positive-leading polynomials. Let \\(\\mathcal G=\\bigcup_i\\mathcal G_i\\), including shared factors only once. The joint local condition implies local admissibility of every \\(F_i\\), hence of every \\(g\\in\\mathcal G\\). Proposition 12 gives the \\(o(X)\\) large-prime tail for each factor.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal B\\) contain prime divisors of all \\(c_i\\), all leading coefficients and discriminants of \\(g\\in\\mathcal G\\), and all resultants of distinct pairs. This is finite, since the discriminants and pairwise resultants are nonzero. For \\(p\\notin\\mathcal B\\), distinct factors cannot vanish at the same residue modulo \\(p\\), and all roots are simple. Hence\n\\[\np^k\\mid F_i(n)\n\\iff p^k\\mid g(n)\\text{ for some }g\\in\\mathcal G_i,\n\\]\nand, with \\(D=\\sum_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\deg g\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\Omega_p\\le\\sum_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\rho_g(p^k)\n=\\sum_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\rho_g(p)\\le D.\n\\]\nOnce \\(X\\) exceeds all primes in \\(\\mathcal B\\), the family tail is contained in the union of finitely many factor tails and has size \\(o_{\\mathbf F,k}(X)\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(Y\\) beyond \\(\\mathcal B\\), the Chinese remainder theorem leaves exactly\n\\[\nQ_Y\\prod_{p\\le Y}\\left(1-\\frac{\\#\\Omega_p}{p^k}\\right)\n\\]\nclasses modulo \\(Q_Y=\\prod_{p\\le Y}p^k\\). Proposition 1's intermediate-prime estimate applies with \\(D\\) in place of \\(d\\). First let \\(X\\to\\infty\\), then \\(Y\\to\\infty\\), and finally pass from dyadic intervals to positive inputs as in Proposition 1.</p><p>The product is positive because its factors are positive and \\(\\sum_p\\#\\Omega_p/p^k&lt;\\infty\\). At primes in \\(\\mathcal B\\), retain the full joint set \\(\\Omega_p\\); valuations from different factors are not separated there. Taking \\(r=1\\) gives the product assertion. Empty products cover nonzero constants, and finitely many integer zeros do not affect the asymptotic.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 12.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Put \\( c_8=\\prod_{p\\equiv1\\;(\\mathrm{mod}\\;8)}\\left(1-\\frac4{p^2}\\right). \\) Then \\(c_8>3/4\\), and \\( \\#\\{1\\le n\\le X:n^4+1\\text{ is squarefree}\\}=c_8X+o(X). \\) Consequently, for every sufficiently large real \\(N\\) …", "statement": "<p>Put\n\\[\nc_8=\\prod_{p\\equiv1\\;(\\mathrm{mod}\\;8)}\\left(1-\\frac4{p^2}\\right).\n\\]\nThen \\(c_8&gt;3/4\\), and\n\\[\n\\#\\{1\\le n\\le X:n^4+1\\text{ is squarefree}\\}=c_8X+o(X).\n\\]\nConsequently, for every sufficiently large real \\(N\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:N\\le n\\le2N,\\ n^4+1\\text{ is squarefree}\\}\\ge N/2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The polynomial \\((T+1)^4+1\\) is Eisenstein at \\(2\\), so \\(T^4+1\\) is irreducible. Its value at zero is \\(1\\), proving local admissibility. There is no root modulo \\(4\\): values are \\(1\\) for even inputs and \\(2\\) for odd inputs.</p><p>For odd \\(p\\), a root of \\(T^4+1\\) has order eight in \\(\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\). Conversely the cyclic group has exactly four elements of order eight if \\(p\\equiv1\\pmod8\\), and none otherwise. Each root is simple, since \\(4T^3\\ne0\\), and lifts uniquely modulo \\(p^2\\). Theorem 1 therefore gives the stated product and asymptotic.</p><p>Every prime in the product is at least \\(17\\). Using \\(\\prod_j(1-a_j)\\ge1-\\sum_ja_j\\) first for finite products and then taking the limit,\n\\[\nc_8\\ge1-4\\sum_{p\\equiv1\\;(\\mathrm{mod}\\;8)}p^{-2}\n&gt;1-4\\sum_{m\\ge17}m^{-2}\n&gt;1-4\\int_{16}^\\infty t^{-2}\\,dt\n=\\frac34.\n\\]\nSubtract the asymptotics at \\(2N\\) and \\(N\\), with an \\(O(1)\\) endpoint correction. The dyadic count is \\(c_8N+o(N)\\), hence at least \\(N/2\\) for sufficiently large \\(N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Proposition 12.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "With \\(\\epsilon_0=10^{-6}\\), there exists \\(\\delta_0>0\\) such that …", "statement": "<p>With \\(\\epsilon_0=10^{-6}\\), there exists \\(\\delta_0&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\#\\left\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:\nN\\le n\\le2N,\\ \np^2\\mid n^4+1\n\\text{ for some prime }\nN^{1-\\epsilon_0}\\le p\\le N^{3/2+\\epsilon_0}\n\\right\\}\n\\ll N^{1-\\delta_0}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 11 already treats \\(p&gt;N\\). For \\(N^{1-\\epsilon_0}\\le p\\le N\\), split into dyadic intervals with lower endpoint \\(P=N^\\eta\\), \\(1-\\epsilon_0\\le\\eta\\le1\\), and enlarge the first quartic cell to\n\\[\n\\eta_-=1-\\epsilon_0,\\quad\n\\eta_+=3001/3000,\\quad\nb_-=2999/1500,\\quad\nb_+=2+2\\epsilon_0,\n\\]\n\\[\nt_*=1/2,\\quad w=24809/100000,\\quad\n\\tau=1/50000.\n\\]\nKeep mode \\(Q\\). These give\n\\[\nm=74427/100000,\\quad\nu=76819/1200000,\\quad\nv=313933/1000000.\n\\]\nBoth strict determinant inequalities hold, \\(m&lt;\\min(\\eta_-,b_-)\\), and \\(3/20\\le v/u\\le5\\).</p><p>The original Appendix A.1 gives the following additional exact checks, using `F` and `upper` from Lemma 10.1:\n“`python\nepsilon = F(1, 10**6)\netaL, etaH = 1-epsilon, F(3001, 3000)\nbL, bH = 4-2*etaH, 2+2*epsilon\nt, w = F(1, 2), F(24809, 100000)\nm = 3*w\nu, v = (etaH-m)/4, (bH-m)/4\nassert 0 &lt; epsilon &lt; F(1, 2)\nassert 0 &lt; m &lt; 1 and m &lt; min(etaL, bL)\nassert w**3*10 &gt; (F(5, 16)*(etaH+bH*t))**4\nassert w**2*5 &gt; ((etaH+bH*t)/3)**3\nassert min(u, v) &gt; 0 and F(3, 20) &lt;= v/u &lt;= 5\ndef quartic_delta(h, D):\n    return upper((u*v)**h/((2*u+v)**(h-1)*D), h)\nP = [quartic_delta(4, u+v), quartic_delta(3, v),\n     max(quartic_delta(2, min(3*u, 2*v, u+v)),\n         upper(u*u/3, 2)), max(u/2, min(u, v/4))]\nassert P == [F(4729, 100000), F(5097, 100000),\n             F(6899, 100000), F(76819, 1200000)]\ngap = 1-m-sum(max(P[:i]) for i in range(1, 5))\ntau = F(1, 50000)\nassert gap-4*tau == F(1941, 100000)\nassert 1-m-2*u-2*tau == F(15319, 120000)\n“`\nHere \\((H,H_a,H_b)=(10,5,10)\\) at \\(t_*=1/2\\). Thus conservative thresholds in decreasing dimension are\n\\[\n(\\overline\\delta_4,\\overline\\delta_3,\\overline\\delta_2,\\delta_1)\n=\n\\left(\\frac{4729}{100000},\n\\frac{5097}{100000},\n\\frac{6899}{100000},\n\\frac{76819}{1200000}\\right).\n\\]\nTheir cumulative maxima give\n\\[\n1-m-\\sum_h\\overline E_h-4\\tau=\\frac{1941}{100000}&gt;0,\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\n1-m-2u-2\\tau=\\frac{15319}{120000}&gt;0.\n\\]\nHence both the lattice sum and infinite-fiber deletion retain fixed power savings.</p><p>It remains to check arithmetic hypotheses, not merely the numerical inequalities. Since \\(\\epsilon_0&lt;1/2\\),\n\\[\np^2\\ge N^{2-2\\epsilon_0}&gt;N,\n\\]\nand \\(p\\to\\infty\\). In Lemma 3.1, the uses of \\(p&gt;X\\) were exclusion of fixed bad primes and at most one input per root class modulo \\(p^2\\) in an interval of length \\(N\\). Both remain valid. Repeating that factorization proof gives the same selected-point multiplicities in this band.</p><p>The paired charts, determinant cuts, and lattice grouping need only the displayed coordinate upper bounds and strict inequalities. The Hilbert and curve alternatives impose no further \\(p&gt;N\\) condition. Therefore the number-field argument of Lemma 11.2 applies throughout the enlarged cell. Delete the one global quintic-value set before summing, absorb \\(O(\\log N)\\) intervals by a smaller saving, and combine with Proposition 11 for \\(p&gt;N\\). The endpoint correction at \\(n=N\\) is \\(O(1)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "sieve transfer and removal of sign and content", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be irreducible over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), of degree \\(d\\ge2\\), and let \\(k\\ge2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be irreducible over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), of degree \\(d\\ge2\\), and let \\(k\\ge2\\). Assume \\(\\rho_f(p^k)&lt;p^k\\) for every prime \\(p\\). Write\n\\[\nf=scg,\\qquad s\\in\\{-1,1\\},\\quad c\\in\\mathbb N,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\) is the positive content and \\(g\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) is primitive with positive leading coefficient.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If\n\\[\nT_g(X):=\\#\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:X&lt;n\\le2X,\\ p^k\\mid g(n)\n\\text{ for some prime }p&gt;X\\}=o_{g,k}(X), \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nS_{f,k}(N)=c_{f,k}N+o_{f,k}(N),\n\\qquad\nc_{f,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^k)}{p^k}\\right)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If instead the density asymptotic\n\\[\nS_{g,k}(N)=c_{g,k}N+o_{g,k}(N),\n\\qquad\nc_{g,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_g(p^k)}{p^k}\\right)\n\\]\nis already known, the same conclusion for \\(f\\), with its original local factors, follows without a separate estimate in arithmetic progressions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.2 proves assertion 2. For assertion 1, neither \\(f\\) nor \\(g\\) has an integer root, because it is irreducible of degree at least two.</p><p>Fix an integer \\(Y\\ge2\\) exceeding every prime in \\(\\mathcal B\\). Let \\(A_Y(X)\\) count the inputs in \\((X,2X]\\) avoiding \\(p^k\\mid f(n)\\) for every \\(p\\le Y\\), and write\n\\[\nc_Y=\\prod_{p\\le Y}\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^k)}{p^k}\\right).\n\\]\nThere are exactly \\(Q_Yc_Y\\) permitted classes modulo \\(Q_Y\\), so\n\\[\nA_Y(X)=c_YX+O(Q_Y). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nHere \\(Y\\) remains fixed as \\(X\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>If \\(A(X)\\) counts all \\(k\\)-power-free values in \\((X,2X]\\), the inputs removed by \\(Y&lt;p\\le X\\) number at most\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_Y(X)\n&amp;\\le\\sum_{Y&lt;p\\le X}\\rho_f(p^k)\\left(\\frac X{p^k}+1\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\le dX\\sum_{m&gt;Y}m^{-k}+d\\pi(X)\\\\\n&amp;\\le\\frac d{k-1}XY^{1-k}+d\\pi(X). \\qquad(2.5)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(X\\), Lemma 1.1 identifies the remaining exceptional set with the one counted by \\(T_g(X)\\). Hence\n\\[\n0\\le A_Y(X)-A(X)\\le I_Y(X)+T_g(X). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\pi(X)\\ll X/\\log X\\), divide by \\(X\\), first let \\(X\\to\\infty\\) with \\(Y\\) fixed, and then let \\(Y\\to\\infty\\). This gives\n\\[\nA(X)=c_{f,k}X+o_{f,k}(X). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(J\\), sum over the disjoint intervals\n\\[\n(N/2^{j+1},N/2^j],\\qquad 0\\le j&lt;J.\n\\]\nTheir total is \\(c_{f,k}N(1-2^{-J})+o_{f,k,J}(N)\\); the omitted interval contains at most \\(N2^{-J}\\) integers. Letting \\(J\\to\\infty\\) proves the positive-input asymptotic.</p><p>As in original Remark 2.2, fixed finite congruence restrictions may be retained: the restricted large-prime exceptional set is a subset of the unrestricted one, and the finite sieve uses the least common multiple of the relevant moduli. No independence of overlapping conditions is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 1, \\(g\\) is irreducible and locally admissible.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, \\(g\\) is irreducible and locally admissible. For \\(p\\mid c\\), let \\(e=v_p(c)\\). Then \\(e&lt;k\\), and\n\\[\n\\rho_f(p^k)=p^e\\rho_g(p^{k-e}),\n\\qquad\n1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^k)}{p^k}\n=\n1-\\frac{\\rho_g(p^{k-e})}{p^{k-e}}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\nmid c\\), the root counts modulo \\(p^k\\) agree.</p><p>There is a finite set \\(\\mathcal B\\), containing the prime divisors of \\(c\\), the leading coefficient of \\(g\\), and the discriminant of \\(g\\), such that\n\\[\n\\rho_f(p^k)=\\rho_g(p^k)=\\rho_g(p)\\le d\n\\qquad(p\\notin\\mathcal B). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nThe Euler products for \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) converge to positive numbers. Once \\(X\\) exceeds every prime divisor of \\(c\\), their large-prime exceptional sets are identical.</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiplication by \\(s\\) does not affect divisibility. Local admissibility forces \\(v_p(c)&lt;k\\). The condition \\(p^k\\mid f(a)\\) is equivalent to \\(p^{k-e}\\mid g(a)\\), and every residue modulo \\(p^{k-e}\\) has \\(p^e\\) lifts modulo \\(p^k\\), proving (2.2).</p><p>Outside \\(\\mathcal B\\), reduction of \\(g\\) has degree \\(d\\) and simple roots. The expansion\n\\[\ng(a+p^ju)\\equiv g(a)+p^ju\\,g'(a)\\pmod{p^{j+1}}\n\\]\ndetermines \\(u\\) uniquely modulo \\(p\\), because \\(g'(a)\\) is invertible there. Thus every root lifts uniquely, proving (2.3).</p><p>For \\(q_p=\\rho_f(p^k)/p^k\\), one has \\(q_p&lt;1\\) and \\(\\sum_pq_p&lt;\\infty\\). For sufficiently large \\(p\\),\n\\[\n-2q_p\\le\\log(1-q_p)\\le-q_p.\n\\]\nHence the product is positive. The same argument applies to \\(g\\). For primes exceeding the divisors of \\(c\\), divisibility by \\(p^k\\) is unchanged on removing the content.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the hypotheses of Proposition 1, assume the stated density asymptotic for \\(g\\).", "statement": "<p>Under the hypotheses of Proposition 1, assume the stated density asymptotic for \\(g\\). For an integer \\(Y\\) exceeding every prime divisor of \\(c\\), let \\(B_{h,Y}(N)\\), for \\(h=f,g\\), count \\(1\\le n\\le N\\) avoiding \\(p^k\\mid h(n)\\) for every \\(p\\le Y\\), and set\n\\[\nc_{h,Y}=\\prod_{p\\le Y}\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_h(p^k)}{p^k}\\right),\n\\qquad Q_Y=\\prod_{p\\le Y}p^k.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n0\\le B_{f,Y}(N)-S_{f,k}(N)\n\\le B_{g,Y}(N)-S_{g,k}(N),\n\\]\nand the density conclusion for \\(f\\) follows.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Chinese remainder theorem gives\n\\[\nB_{h,Y}(N)=c_{h,Y}N+O(Q_Y).\n\\]\nAn input counted by \\(B_{f,Y}-S_{f,k}\\) avoids the small-prime conditions for \\(f\\), hence those for \\(g\\), but has a divisor \\(p^k\\) with \\(p&gt;Y\\). Since \\(p\\nmid c\\), that divisor also divides \\(g(n)\\). This proves the inequality.</p><p>For fixed \\(Y\\), divide by \\(N\\) and let \\(N\\to\\infty\\). The assumed density of \\(g\\) yields\n\\[\nc_{f,Y}-(c_{g,Y}-c_{g,k})\n\\le\\liminf_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{S_{f,k}(N)}N\n\\le\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{S_{f,k}(N)}N\n\\le c_{f,Y}.\n\\]\nLet \\(Y\\to\\infty\\), using Lemma 1.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "affine counting with adaptive auxiliary primes", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix integers \\(1\\le r\\le N\\), a degree bound \\(e_0\\), a constant \\(C\\), positive weights \\(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_N\\), and positive numbers \\(\\delta_1,\\ldots,\\delta_r\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix integers \\(1\\le r\\le N\\), a degree bound \\(e_0\\), a constant \\(C\\), positive weights \\(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_N\\), and positive numbers \\(\\delta_1,\\ldots,\\delta_r\\). Degree means ordinary degree of projective closure, summed over irreducible components for a reducible set; irreducibility is over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>For \\(X\\ge2\\), let \\(S\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^N\\) satisfy\n\\[\n|z_l|\\le CX^{\\omega_l},\n\\]\nand suppose \\(S\\) lies on an algebraic set \\(Z\\subseteq\\mathbb C^N\\) of degree at most \\(e_0\\) and dimension at most \\(r\\). Define\n\\[\nH_A(T)=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\operatorname{span}\n\\{z^\\alpha|_A:\\omega\\cdot\\alpha\\le T\\}.\n\\]\nAssume that, for every fixed degree bound \\(e\\), each irreducible \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined subvariety \\(A\\subseteq Z\\), of degree at most \\(e\\), dimension \\(h&gt;0\\), and meeting \\(S\\), satisfies either\n\\[\n|S\\cap A|\\ll_{e,\\alpha}X^\\alpha\n\\quad\\text{for every fixed }\\alpha&gt;0, \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nH_A(T)\\ge\\frac{T^h}{h!\\delta_h^h}-C_eT^{h-1}\n\\qquad(T\\ge1). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nAll constants in these alternatives are uniform in \\(X,Z,S,A\\).</p><p>Put \\(E_h=\\max_{h\\le s\\le r}\\delta_s\\). Then, for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n|S|\\ll_\\varepsilon X^{E_1+\\cdots+E_r+\\varepsilon}. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nNo bound on the coefficients initially defining \\(Z\\) is required. The conclusion is uniform over families of weights when their positive lower bounds, upper bounds, and the constants in (3.2) are uniform.</p>", "proof": "<p>Zero-dimensional sets contain at most their degree many points, so assume \\(S\\ne\\varnothing\\). Use the full paths of Lemma 2.3. Put \\(E_{r+1}=0\\) and\n\\[\na_i=E_{h_i}-E_{h_i+1}\\ge0.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\xi&gt;0\\) so that\n\\[\n\\xi(E_1+r\\gamma)&lt;\\gamma/2. \\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nTake \\(T_0\\) sufficiently large in terms of \\(r,B_0,\\xi\\), then choose \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) with \\(\\zeta(T_0+1)\\le\\gamma\\). Chebyshev bounds provide disjoint prime pools of cardinality tending to infinity between \\(X^\\zeta\\) and \\(C_rX^\\zeta\\), for a sufficiently large fixed \\(C_r\\).</p><p>Sample block \\(i\\) with sizes beginning at\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\\left\\lceil\\frac{a_i+\\gamma}{\\zeta}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nEvery allowed size \\(t_i\\) satisfies\n\\[\na_i+\\gamma\\le\\zeta t_i\\le a_i+2\\gamma. \\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nCall \\(z\\) regular if in each block at most a fraction \\(\\xi\\) of the primes divide any full-path minor. Lemma 2.3, Markov's inequality, and a union bound give\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(z\\text{ is not regular})\n\\le\\frac r\\xi(B_0/T_0+o(1))&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]\nSome fixed block choice therefore makes at least \\(|S|/2\\) points regular.</p><p>For a regular point, the product of primes in the first \\(i\\) blocks avoiding its current parent minor is at least\n\\[\nX^{(1-\\xi)\\zeta\\sum_{j\\le i}t_j}\n\\ge X^{(1-\\xi)(E_{h_i}+i\\gamma)}\n\\ge X^{\\delta_{h_i}+\\gamma/2}. \\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nEarlier primes remain available even though the current parent depends on them.</p><p>Arrange regular paths in a tree. Stop at parents satisfying (3.1), or at zero-dimensional parents. Inductively, a parent at step \\(i\\) has dimension at most \\(h_i\\). For a patch of dimension \\(h\\le h_i\\), its reductions modulo \\(q\\) with \\(\\Delta\\ne0\\) number \\(O(q^h)\\): fix the free coordinates and use Bézout to bound the isolated solutions of the remaining square system with invertible Jacobian. No good reduction of the whole variety is required.</p><p>Allow every subset of at most \\(\\xi t_i\\) new primes where the parent minor vanishes. There are only constantly many subsets. At bad primes use \\(q^N\\), and at the others use \\(O(q^{h_i})\\). The number of new simultaneous classes is\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(X^{\\zeta t_i(h_i+N\\xi)}\\right)\n\\le O\\!\\left(X^{(a_i+2\\gamma)(h_i+N\\xi)}\\right). \\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nEarlier congruences can only decrease this number.</p><p>In a class containing a regular point, if the parent has dimension exactly \\(h_i\\), (3.10) and Lemma 2.2 give a polynomial in \\(K_i\\) nonzero on the parent. It vanishes on the entire class in \\(S\\cap A\\), not merely its regular points. Consequently the child intersection has dimension at most \\(h_i-1\\). If the parent already had smaller dimension, its children automatically satisfy this bound.</p><p>After \\(r\\) stages, nonexceptional leaves are zero-dimensional. The total branching exponent is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^rh_ia_i\n+2\\gamma\\sum_{i=1}^rh_i\n+N\\xi(E_1+2r\\gamma). \\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nThe first sum telescopes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{h=1}^rh(E_h-E_{h+1})=\\sum_{h=1}^rE_h.\n\\]\nEvery prefix has no larger exponent. Exceptional stopping parents contribute only an additional \\(O(X^\\alpha)\\).</p><p>Given \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), first choose \\(\\gamma\\) making the second term below \\(\\varepsilon/4\\); then fix degree limits and \\(B_0\\); then choose \\(\\xi\\) satisfying (3.8) and making the last term below \\(\\varepsilon/4\\). Choose \\(\\alpha&lt;\\varepsilon/4\\), then \\(T_0,\\zeta\\), and finally sufficiently large \\(X\\). All constants are fixed before \\(X\\to\\infty\\). The bound applies to at least half of \\(S\\), and multiplication by two proves (3.3).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "smooth patches with controlled equations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the box setting of Proposition 2, let \\(Z'\\subseteq\\mathbb C^N\\) have degree at most \\(e\\).", "statement": "<p>In the box setting of Proposition 2, let \\(Z'\\subseteq\\mathbb C^N\\) have degree at most \\(e\\). A patch is a triple \\((A,G,\\Delta)\\), where \\(A\\subseteq Z'\\) is irreducible and \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined of dimension \\(h\\), \\(G\\) is a list of \\(N-h\\) integer polynomials vanishing on \\(A\\), and \\(\\Delta\\) is a square Jacobian minor in \\(N-h\\) coordinate columns. It covers the points of \\(A\\) where \\(\\Delta\\ne0\\). If \\(h=N\\), take the empty list and \\(\\Delta=1\\).</p><p>There are boundedly many such patches covering \\(S\\cap Z'\\). Their degrees are bounded, and their coefficients are bounded by \\(C'X^{C'}\\), with constants depending only on \\(e,N,C\\) and the weights. At a covered point \\(z\\), \\(\\Delta(z)\\) is a nonzero integer of uniformly polynomial size. The construction can be fixed as a function of \\(Z'\\), its degree allowance, and \\(S\\) alone.</p>", "proof": "<p>Split \\(Z'\\) into irreducible components. If \\(A\\) is not defined over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), choose an automorphism \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\mathbb C\\) fixing \\(\\mathbb Q\\) with \\(\\sigma(A)\\ne A\\). Every rational point of \\(A\\) lies in the proper intersection \\(A\\cap\\sigma(A)\\). Replace \\(A\\) by its bounded-degree components and continue in smaller dimension.</p><p>For the descent assertion, if every such automorphism fixed \\(A\\), it would preserve every bounded-degree equation space. Its reduced row-echelon basis in ordered monomial coordinates is unique, hence rational, giving rational equations for \\(A\\). The fixed field is \\(\\mathbb Q\\): a nonrational algebraic number has a conjugate, while a transcendental number can be moved in a transcendence basis, and these maps extend to automorphisms of \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>Now suppose \\(A\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined of dimension \\(h\\) and degree \\(e'\\). Its equations of degree at most \\(e'\\) have generic Jacobian rank \\(N-h\\). Choose \\(h\\) algebraically independent coordinate functions and project onto them together with each remaining coordinate in turn. Each image closure is an irreducible hypersurface of degree at most \\(e'\\), whose derivative in the extra coordinate is nonzero by characteristic-zero separability. The lifted equations supply a diagonal generically nonzero minor.</p><p>Evaluate all degree-\\(\\le e'\\) monomials on \\(S\\cap A\\). The integer matrix has boundedly many columns and polynomially bounded entries. Its nullspace has an integer spanning set of polynomial height: select independent rows and pivot columns, solve by cofactors, and clear the pivot determinant.</p><p>The complexified nullspace contains the degree-\\(\\le e'\\) ideal space of \\(A\\). If containment is strict, one bounded-height spanning polynomial is nonzero on \\(A\\) and vanishes on \\(S\\cap A\\); intersect with it and recurse. If equality holds, choose \\(N-h\\) spanning equations with a generically nonzero minor \\(\\Delta\\), use the resulting patch, and recurse on \\(A\\cap\\{\\Delta=0\\}\\). For a rational zero-dimensional point \\(z\\), use the equations \\(z_l'-z_l\\) and minor \\(1\\).</p><p>Every intersection is proper. Dimension decreases along every recursive branch, and Bézout controls the number, degrees, and height exponents. Fix all choices and orderings deterministically from the stated inputs. A prime affecting the input variety is not separately consulted when selecting its patches.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "a determinant cut in a simultaneous residue class", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(h\\ge1\\), let \\(s_h(L)\\) be the sum of the first \\(L\\) total degrees of monomials in \\(h\\) variables, counted with multiplicity.", "statement": "<p>For \\(h\\ge1\\), let \\(s_h(L)\\) be the sum of the first \\(L\\) total degrees of monomials in \\(h\\) variables, counted with multiplicity. Then\n\\[\ns_h(L)=\\frac h{h+1}(h!)^{1/h}L^{1+1/h}+O_h(L). \\tag{3.4}\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(\\nu&gt;0\\) and bounds on the degrees of a patch \\((A,G,\\Delta)\\) and its equations, where \\(h=\\dim A&gt;0\\). Assume (3.2). Let \\(z\\in S\\cap A\\) be covered by the patch, and let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be distinct primes such that\n\\[\nq\\nmid\\Delta(z)\\quad(q\\in\\mathcal Q),\n\\qquad\n\\prod_{q\\in\\mathcal Q}q\\ge X^{\\delta_h+\\nu}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(X\\), all points of \\(S\\cap A\\) congruent to \\(z\\) modulo these primes lie on a polynomial of bounded degree that is not identically zero on \\(A\\). Additional congruence conditions may be imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Counting lattice points in a simplex gives (3.4). Fix \\(q\\nmid\\Delta(z)\\), translate by \\(z\\), and use as free coordinates the \\(h\\) columns outside the minor. The implicit equations \\(G=0\\) solve the other coordinates as power series with coefficients in \\(\\mathbb Z_q\\), convergent on \\(q\\mathbb Z_q^h\\). Hensel uniqueness gives every solution congruent to \\(z\\).</p><p>An \\(L\\times L\\) determinant of monomial values in that residue class has \\(q\\)-adic valuation at least \\(s_h(L)\\). Expanding in the free differences, terms with repeated expansion monomials vanish; \\(L\\) distinct monomials have total degree at least \\(s_h(L)\\). Truncation modulo arbitrary powers of \\(q\\) justifies the series argument.</p><p>From (3.2), independent coordinate monomials can be chosen successively with \\(j\\)-th weight at most\n\\[\n\\delta_h(h!j)^{1/h}+O(1).\n\\]\nIndeed substitute \\(T=\\delta_h(h!j)^{1/h}+C_1\\) and take \\(C_1\\) sufficiently large. Their first \\(L\\) weights sum to at most \\(\\delta_hs_h(L)+O(L)\\). Thus the integer evaluation determinant has absolute value at most\n\\[\nC_LX^{\\delta_hs_h(L)+C_2L},\n\\]\nwhile it is divisible by \\((\\prod_{q\\in\\mathcal Q}q)^{s_h(L)}\\). Choose fixed \\(L\\) so that \\(\\nu s_h(L)&gt;C_2L\\), then take \\(X\\) large. Every determinant vanishes.</p><p>The evaluation map has deficient rank. A nonzero rational linear combination of the chosen independent monomials vanishes on the class but not on \\(A\\). Its ordinary degree is bounded because its weight and the positive minimum coordinate weight are bounded.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "deletion estimate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix a small \\(\\gamma>0\\), put \\(h_i=r+1-i\\), and choose successive degree limits \\(D_i\\) large enough for Lemma 2.2 in parent dimension \\(h_i\\), with \\(\\nu=\\gamma/2\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a small \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), put \\(h_i=r+1-i\\), and choose successive degree limits \\(D_i\\) large enough for Lemma 2.2 in parent dimension \\(h_i\\), with \\(\\nu=\\gamma/2\\). Include all degree bounds obtained by intersections and Lemma 2.1, even on paths where dimension does not decrease.</p><p>For disjoint finite prime blocks \\(B_1,\\ldots,B_r\\), write\n\\[\nQ_i=\\prod_{j\\le i}\\prod_{q\\in B_j}q.\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\in S\\), take the first initial patch covering \\(z\\). Given its parent \\(A_{i-1}\\), define\n\\[\nK_i=\n\\{F\\in\\mathbb Q[z_1,\\ldots,z_N]_{\\le D_i}:\nF(z')=0\\text{ for all }z'\\in S\\cap A_{i-1},\\\nz'\\equiv z\\pmod{Q_i}\\}. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nRecord its codimension \\(\\rho_i\\), and take the first patch covering \\(z\\) in \\(A_{i-1}\\cap V(K_i)\\). Construct all \\(r\\) stages, without stopping at exceptional or zero-dimensional parents.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nM_i=\\binom{N+D_i}{N},\n\\qquad\nR=\\sum_{i=1}^r\\rho_i\\prod_{j&gt;i}(M_j+1),\n\\qquad\n0\\le R&lt;B_0:=\\prod_{i=1}^r(M_i+1). \\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(z\\), all blocks except the \\(i\\)-th, and a disjoint prime pool \\(\\mathcal P_i\\) whose primes lie between \\(X^\\zeta\\) and \\(C_rX^\\zeta\\), where \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) is fixed and \\(|\\mathcal P_i|\\to\\infty\\). Choose the block size uniformly among \\(T_0\\) consecutive positive integers, independently of \\(X\\), then choose the block uniformly among subsets of that size. The expected fraction of its primes dividing at least one full-path integer\n\\[\n\\Delta_0(z),\\ldots,\\Delta_r(z)\n\\]\nis at most \\(B_0/T_0+o(1)\\), uniformly in \\(z\\) and the other blocks.</p>", "proof": "<p>Adding a prime makes the rank vector \\((\\rho_1,\\ldots,\\rho_r)\\) lexicographically nonincreasing. Until the first changed entry, parent patches agree: residue classes shrink, kernels are nested, and equal codimensions force equal kernels. Deterministic patch choices then agree. The first changed codimension can only decrease. Equality of the entire vector forces equality of all kernels, patches, and evaluated minors.</p><p>Delete a uniformly chosen prime \\(q\\) from the block. If \\(F(t)\\) is the expected record for a uniform \\(t\\)-subset, the expected increase under deletion is \\(F(t-1)-F(t)\\). It is a nonnegative integer, at least one if the record changes. Averaging over sizes \\(\\ell,\\ldots,\\ell+T_0-1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\text{record changes})\n\\le\n\\frac{F(\\ell-1)-F(\\ell+T_0-1)}{T_0}\n\\le\\frac{B_0}{T_0}. \\tag{3.7}\n\\]</p><p>Condition on the deleted subset and other blocks. Its path minors are fixed nonzero integers of uniformly polynomial size. Their product has only \\(O(1/\\zeta)\\) prime divisors at least \\(X^\\zeta\\). The removed prime is uniform in a complement whose cardinality tends to infinity, so its probability of dividing that product is \\(o(1)\\), uniformly.</p><p>Without conditioning on stability,\n\\[\n\\{q\\text{ divides a full-path minor}\\}\n\\subseteq\n\\{\\text{record changes}\\}\n\\cup\n\\{q\\text{ divides a deleted-path minor}\\}.\n\\]\nOn record stability the minors agree. This gives the asserted probability, which is exactly the expected bad fraction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Arithmetic factorization and paired boxes; original Lemmas 4.1–4.2.] Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[T]\\) be primitive and irreducible, of degree \\(d\\), with positive leading coefficient \\(c\\), and put \\(k=d-2\\ge2\\).", "statement": "<p>[Arithmetic factorization and paired boxes; original Lemmas 4.1–4.2.]</p><p>Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[T]\\) be primitive and irreducible, of degree \\(d\\), with positive leading coefficient \\(c\\), and put \\(k=d-2\\ge2\\). Fix a root \\(\\theta\\), let \\(K=\\mathbb Q(\\theta)\\), let \\(L\\) be a splitting field, and write \\(\\theta_i=\\sigma_i(\\theta)\\) for the \\(d\\) embeddings \\(\\sigma_i:K\\hookrightarrow L\\).</p><p>Consider\n\\[\nX&lt;n\\le2X,\\quad P\\le p\\le2P,\\quad p&gt;X,\\quad p^k\\mid f(n),\n\\quad P=X^\\eta,\\quad b=d-k\\eta.\n\\]\nChoose one representation for each input under consideration.</p><p>There are finitely many arithmetic and maximal-coordinate charts in which the selected inputs give integer vectors \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\) and linear forms \\(a_i(x),b_i(y)\\) satisfying\n\\[\na_i(x)^kb_i(y)=n-\\theta_i,\n\\qquad\n|a_i(x)|\\asymp_fX^{\\eta/d},\n\\qquad\n|b_i(y)|\\asymp_fX^{b/d}. \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nThere is at most one selected point for each \\(n\\), and a fixed \\(x\\) costs \\(O_f(1)\\) selected points.</p><p>For \\(0&lt;w&lt;1\\), choose an integer \\(M\\asymp X^w\\). In a maximal-coordinate chart, put\n\\[\ns_j=x_j/x_0,\\quad t_j=y_j/y_0\\quad(1\\le j&lt;d),\n\\qquad z=1/n.\n\\]\nPartition both direction cubes into boxes of side \\(M^{-1}\\). There are \\(O_f(M^{d-1})\\) relevant pairs, and each box has \\(O_f(1)\\) partners in the other direction. On boundedly many branches, either direction is analytic in the other and \\(z\\), with fixed-radius complex neighborhoods and uniformly bounded derivatives of every fixed order.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 3.1 and express the integral factors in the fixed basis. Invertibility of the embedding matrices gives the coordinate bounds and maximal-coordinate charts in Lemma 3.2. Equation (4.1) becomes (4.4). The selected-point multiplicities follow from the selection of one representation per input and the fixed-\\(\\alpha\\) bound. Lemma 3.2 gives the paired-box conclusions. Both multiplicities survive invertible integral changes in the two coordinate blocks.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "balanced factorization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the arithmetic hypotheses of Proposition 3, there are a positive integer \\(\\mu\\) and a fixed finite list of integral ideals \\(\\mathfrak c\\) of \\(K\\) such that every sufficiently large-\\(X\\) pair \\((n,p)\\) admits …", "statement": "<p>Under the arithmetic hypotheses of Proposition 3, there are a positive integer \\(\\mu\\) and a fixed finite list of integral ideals \\(\\mathfrak c\\) of \\(K\\) such that every sufficiently large-\\(X\\) pair \\((n,p)\\) admits \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathcal O_K\\) with\n\\[\n\\alpha^k\\beta=\\mu(n-\\theta),\n\\qquad\n|N_{K/\\mathbb Q}(\\alpha)|=pN(\\mathfrak c), \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|\\sigma_i(\\alpha)|\\asymp_fP^{1/d},\n\\qquad\n|\\sigma_i(\\beta)/\\mu|\\asymp_fX/P^{k/d} \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nfor every embedding. For a fixed ideal case and fixed \\(\\alpha\\), there are at most \\(d\\) selected inputs \\(n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The element \\(c\\theta\\) is integral, with monic minimal polynomial\n\\[\nF(T)=c^{d-1}f(T/c),\n\\qquad\nN_{K/\\mathbb Q}(cn-c\\theta)=F(cn)=c^{d-1}f(n). \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nExclude primes dividing \\(c\\), the discriminant of \\(F\\), or \\([\\mathcal O_K:\\mathbb Z[c\\theta]]\\). For every remaining prime dividing \\(f(n)\\), exactly one prime ideal \\(\\mathfrak p\\) above \\(p\\) contains \\(cn-c\\theta\\): its residue factor is the simple linear factor \\(T-cn\\). Thus \\(N(\\mathfrak p)=p\\), and its valuation in \\(cn-c\\theta\\) equals \\(v_p(f(n))\\). In particular \\(\\mathfrak p^k\\mid(cn-c\\theta)\\).</p><p>Choose fixed integral class representatives. One \\(\\mathfrak c\\) makes \\(\\mathfrak p\\mathfrak c\\) principal; write\n\\[\n(\\alpha)=\\mathfrak p\\mathfrak c.\n\\]\nBalance its generator by a unit. Use logarithmic coordinates at real embeddings and complex pairs with weights \\(1,2\\), subtract \\(d^{-1}\\log(pN(\\mathfrak c))\\) from every coordinate, and move the resulting sum-zero vector into a bounded fundamental parallelepiped of the logarithmic unit lattice. This gives the first estimate in (4.2).</p><p>Choose \\(q&gt;0\\) with \\(q\\mathfrak c^{-k}\\subseteq\\mathcal O_K\\) for every listed ideal, and set \\(\\mu=cq\\). Since \\((cn-c\\theta)\\mathfrak p^{-k}\\) is integral,\n\\[\n\\beta=\\frac{\\mu(n-\\theta)}{\\alpha^k}\n\\]\nis integral. As \\(|n-\\theta_i|\\asymp_fX\\), the second balance follows.</p><p>Choose one prime, ideal case, and balanced generator per selected \\(n\\). Fixed \\(\\alpha\\) determines \\(p\\). Outside the excluded primes, there are at most \\(d\\) roots modulo \\(p^k\\); since \\(p^k&gt;X\\), each root class meets \\((X,2X]\\) at most once.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "paired boxes", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix an integral basis \\(\\omega_0,\\ldots,\\omega_{d-1}\\) of \\(K\\), write \\( \\alpha=\\sum_jx_j\\omega_j,\\qquad \\beta=\\sum_jy_j\\omega_j, \\) and define …", "statement": "<p>Fix an integral basis \\(\\omega_0,\\ldots,\\omega_{d-1}\\) of \\(K\\), write\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\sum_jx_j\\omega_j,\\qquad \\beta=\\sum_jy_j\\omega_j,\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\na_i(x)=\\sum_j\\sigma_i(\\omega_j)x_j,\n\\qquad\nb_i(y)=\\mu^{-1}\\sum_j\\sigma_i(\\omega_j)y_j.\n\\]\nChoose largest absolute coordinates \\(x_0,y_0\\), after reindexing. Then\n\\[\n|x_0|\\asymp_fP^{1/d},\n\\qquad\n|y_0|\\asymp_fX/P^{k/d},\n\\]\nand every normalized conjugate form \\(a_i(1,s),b_i(1,t)\\) is bounded above and away from zero.</p><p>The analytic and partner conclusions of Proposition 3 hold uniformly. No primality assumption on \\(M\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(A,B\\) be the invertible embedding maps from chart coordinates, and put\n\\[\n\\kappa=x_0^ky_0/n,\\qquad |\\kappa|\\asymp_f1.\n\\]\nThe normalized identities give\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1-\\theta_i z}{a_i(1,s)^k}\\right)_{i=1}^d\n=\\kappa B(1,t). \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nApply \\(B^{-1}\\) and divide by the first coordinate, which equals \\(\\kappa\\) at the counted point, to obtain \\(t\\).</p><p>Conversely take local \\(k\\)-th roots of\n\\[\n\\frac{1-\\theta_i z}{b_i(1,t)}.\n\\]\nTheir inputs lie in a fixed annulus separated from zero. Compatible roots at a counted point are \\(\\gamma a_i(1,s)\\), with \\(\\gamma^k=\\kappa\\). Applying \\(A^{-1}\\) and normalizing its first coordinate recovers \\(s\\). There are at most \\(k^d\\) root choices; retain the branches describing real coordinate vectors.</p><p>The fixed upper and positive lower bounds permit fixed-radius complex polydiscs on which the roots and rational operations are holomorphic. Formulas or Cauchy's estimates bound derivatives of each fixed order uniformly. The grid base with \\(z=0\\) is at distance \\(O(M^{-1}+X^{-1})\\), and therefore lies in the same neighborhood for large \\(X\\).</p><p>Since \\(w&lt;1\\), the allowed \\(z\\)-interval has length \\(O(M^{-1})\\). The forward derivative bound maps one \\(s\\)-box and that interval into a set of \\(t\\)-diameter \\(O_f(M^{-1})\\), meeting boundedly many boxes. For a fixed \\(t\\)-box, continue its root choices to the common base. Each of the boundedly many relevant branches has uniformly nonzero normalization and \\(s\\)-image of diameter \\(O_f(M^{-1})\\). This proves the reverse partner bound. There are \\(O(M^{d-1})\\) boxes in either cube.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "bihomogeneous determinant cuts", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Retain \\(d\\), \\(k=d-2\\), and the distinct roots \\(\\theta_i\\).", "statement": "<p>Retain \\(d\\), \\(k=d-2\\), and the distinct roots \\(\\theta_i\\). Let \\(\\mathcal B\\subseteq\\mathbb C^{2d}\\) be defined by\n\\[\n(a_i^kb_i)_{i=1}^d\\in\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\\{(1)_i,(\\theta_i)_i\\},\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\times\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\) be its biprojective variety. Write\n\\[\na_i^kb_i=N_0-\\theta_iL_0.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nR(A,B)=\\frac1{d!}\\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}\n\\binom dj\\binom{d-2}{j-1}k^{j-1}A^{d-j}B^j, \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\\[\nH(t)=d!R(1,t),\\quad\nH_a(t)=(d-1)!(\\partial_AR)(1,t),\\quad\nH_b(t)=(d-1)!(\\partial_BR)(1,t).\n\\]</p><p>Consider a paired box of Proposition 3, with\n\\[\n|x_i|\\ll X^{\\eta_+/d},\\qquad |y_i|\\ll X^{b_+/d},\n\\]\ndirection widths \\(O(X^{-w})\\), \\(z=1/n=O(X^{-1})\\), and its uniform analytic branches. Fix positive rational \\(t_*,w\\) satisfying\n\\[\nw^{d-1}H(t_*)&gt;\n\\left(\\frac{(d+1)(\\eta_++b_+t_*)}{d^2}\\right)^d,\n\\]\n\\[\nw^{d-2}\\min\\{H_a(t_*),H_b(t_*)\\}&gt;\n\\left(\\frac{\\eta_++b_+t_*}{d-1}\\right)^{d-1}. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(X\\), all selected points lie on boundedly many bounded-degree sets of either type:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. a mixed hypersurface section of \\(\\mathcal V\\), whose bicone is a prime complete intersection with one additional equation of bidegree \\((\\lambda,s_0)\\), where \\(\\lambda,s_0\\ge1\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. a set of biprojective dimension at most \\(d-2\\).</p><p>On the affine equations \\(a_i^kb_i=n-\\theta_i\\), their dimensions are respectively \\(d\\) and at most \\(d-1\\). All number and degree bounds are uniform.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose large fixed bidegrees \\(A,B\\) with \\(B/A=t_*\\), and a basis of raw integral-coordinate monomials on \\(\\mathcal V\\). By Lemma 4.1,\n\\[\n\\ell=\\frac{H(t_*)}{d!}A^d+O(A^{d-1}).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell\\) counted points, the raw determinant is integral. After dividing each row by \\(x_0^Ay_0^B\\), a nonzero determinant has absolute value at least\n\\[\nc_{A,B}X^{-\\ell A(\\eta_++b_+t_*)/d}. \\tag{5.5}\n\\]</p><p>Without an additional relation, small-variable monomials number\n\\[\n\\frac{T^d}{d!w^{d-1}}+O(T^{d-1}+1).\n\\]\nLemma 4.2 gives\n\\[\n\\frac S\\ell=\n\\frac d{d+1}\\bigl(w^{d-1}H(t_*)\\bigr)^{1/d}A+O(1).\n\\]\nThe first inequality in (5.2) contradicts (5.5), first for sufficiently large fixed \\(A,B\\), then large \\(X\\). Every determinant vanishes. The evaluation kernel gives a bihomogeneous equation nonzero on \\(\\mathcal V\\), also if fewer than \\(\\ell\\) points occur.</p><p>Substitute \\(b_i=(N_0-\\theta_iL_0)/a_i^k\\), clear denominators, and factor. Lemma 4.3 describes the relevant components. Those with \\(\\lambda,s_0\\ge1\\) are retained as mixed components.</p><p>For a pure component, let its degree in its single block be \\(c_0&gt;0\\). Use the \\(s\\)-chart for a pure \\(a\\)-equation and the \\(t\\)-chart for a pure \\(b\\)-equation. In the latter case partition into the boundedly many reverse branches at the common base, so each determinant uses a single analytic map. Dehomogenization and translation give an exact relation\n\\[\nQ_0(u_1,\\ldots,u_{d-1})=0,\\qquad 1\\le\\deg Q_0\\le c_0.\n\\]\nA nonzero constant relation gives no points.</p><p>Put \\(r=d-1\\), \\(e=\\deg Q_0\\). Multiplication by \\(Q_0\\) injects polynomials of degree at most \\(q-e\\) into those of degree at most \\(q\\), with image evaluating to zero. Thus the evaluation rank is at most\n\\[\nD_e(q)=\\binom{q+r}{r}-\\binom{q-e+r}{r},\n\\]\nthe second term being zero for \\(q&lt;e\\). Apply this at each power \\(z^j\\), with \\(q=\\lfloor(T-j)/w\\rfloor\\). Since\n\\[\nD_e(q)=\\frac{eq^{r-1}}{(r-1)!}+O_{r,c_0}((q+1)^{r-2}),\n\\]\nthe total rank is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{c_0}{r!w^{r-1}}T^r+O(T^{r-1}+1).\n\\]\nThis estimate contains no coefficients of the moving relation.</p><p>For sufficiently large new \\(A,B\\) with the same ratio, a monomial basis on a pure \\(a\\)-component has size\n\\[\n\\ell=\\frac{c_0H_a(t_*)}{r!}A^r+O(A^{r-1}).\n\\]\nLemma 4.2 gives\n\\[\n\\frac S\\ell\\ge\n\\frac rd\\bigl(w^{r-1}H_a(t_*)\\bigr)^{1/r}A-O(1);\n\\]\nthe factor \\(c_0\\) cancels. A pure \\(b\\)-component gives the same expression with \\(H_b\\). The second inequality in (5.2) contradicts (5.5), producing a proper second cut. Pure components therefore have biprojective dimension at most \\(d-2\\). All degrees are fixed uniformly.</p><p>On the counted affine locus \\(L_0=1\\), the map to \\(\\mathcal V\\) has one-dimensional scaling fibers\n\\[\na\\mapsto ca,\\qquad b\\mapsto c^{-k}b.\n\\]\nThus mixed affine components have dimension \\(d\\), and second-cut components dimension at most \\(d-1\\).</p><p>For later use, the mixed equation \\(F(a,n,1)\\) is irreducible, has degree exactly \\(s_0\\ge1\\) in \\(n\\), and its coefficient polynomials in \\(a\\) have no common nonconstant factor. Otherwise \\(F\\) would factor. Their common zero set therefore has codimension at least two whenever nonempty.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The defining ideal of \\(\\mathcal B\\) is prime and is a complete intersection of \\(d-2\\) equations of bidegree \\((k,1)\\).", "statement": "<p>The defining ideal of \\(\\mathcal B\\) is prime and is a complete intersection of \\(d-2\\) equations of bidegree \\((k,1)\\). The dimension of \\(\\mathcal V\\) is \\(d\\). As \\(A,B\\to\\infty\\) at a fixed positive rational ratio \\(B/A\\), its bihomogeneous coordinate space has dimension\n\\[\nR(A,B)+O(A^{d-1}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Where all \\(a_i\\ne0\\), the coordinates \\(a_i,N_0,L_0\\) are free and determine \\(b_i\\), giving an integral smooth open of dimension \\(d+2\\). If \\(a_i=0\\) and the product vector is nonzero, then \\(N_0=\\theta_iL_0\\), \\(L_0\\ne0\\), and every other \\(a_j\\ne0\\). This stratum has dimension \\(d+1\\). If the product vector is zero, the locus is a union of coordinate \\(d\\)-planes.</p><p>Thus the equations have expected codimension \\(d-2\\), and a unique top-dimensional component meeting the smooth open. The complete-intersection criterion used here and below is the following: homogeneous equations of expected codimension in a polynomial ring form a regular sequence; their quotient is equidimensional and Cohen–Macaulay, with no embedded components. A unique top-dimensional component with generic reducedness therefore gives a reduced domain. The paper obtains this by localization at the homogeneous maximal ideal and the cited Stacks Project criteria; the same reasoning applies to positive weighted homogeneous equations.</p><p>Passing to two projective factors subtracts two dimensions. The Hilbert series is\n\\[\n\\frac{(1-z_1^kz_2)^{d-2}}{(1-z_1)^d(1-z_2)^d}.\n\\]\nIts bidegree coefficient is the \\((d-2)\\)-fold finite difference, with step \\((k,1)\\), of\n\\[\n\\binom{A+d-1}{d-1}\\binom{B+d-1}{d-1}.\n\\]\nThe leading term is\n\\[\n(k\\partial_A+\\partial_B)^{d-2}\n\\frac{A^{d-1}B^{d-1}}{((d-1)!)^2},\n\\]\nwhich expands to (5.1); the remaining terms have total degree at most \\(d-1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "[Uniform Taylor determinant estimate, original Section 5.2.] Let \\(\\ell\\) analytic column functions have uniformly bounded derivatives on the chart neighborhoods.", "statement": "<p>[Uniform Taylor determinant estimate, original Section 5.2.]</p><p>Let \\(\\ell\\) analytic column functions have uniformly bounded derivatives on the chart neighborhoods. Give the small variables \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_{d-1},z\\) weights \\(w,\\ldots,w,1\\). Suppose that, on a fixed finite set of evaluation points, the evaluation rank of monomials of weight at most \\(T\\) is at most\n\\[\naT^r+O(T^{r-1}+1),\\qquad a&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell\\) independent monomial evaluation columns with ordered weights \\(\\lambda_j\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}\\lambda_j\n\\ge\\frac r{r+1}a^{-1/r}\\ell^{1+1/r}-O(\\ell). \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nAt points with the stated small-variable bounds, the analytic evaluation determinant satisfies\n\\[\n|\\det|\\ll X^{-S},\n\\qquad\nS=\\frac r{r+1}a^{-1/r}\\ell^{1+1/r}-O(\\ell). \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nUniformity requires only the rank estimate and ambient analytic bounds, not coefficients expressing dependencies.</p>", "proof": "<p>The rank estimate gives\n\\[\nj\\le a\\lambda_j^r+O(\\lambda_j^{r-1}+1).\n\\]\nInvert and sum to obtain (5.3).</p><p>Expand each analytic column at the box base in the original monomials, to fixed total order \\(K\\). A summand in the determinant expansion vanishes if its monomial evaluation columns are dependent. Otherwise (5.3) bounds its total weight, giving \\(X\\) to the negative of that weight. Taylor coefficients are uniformly bounded.</p><p>A remainder column is \\(O(X^{-\\min(w,1)(K+1)})\\). After fixing \\(\\ell\\) and column degrees, choose \\(K\\) so large that every term containing a remainder has a stronger bound than required. Only boundedly many terms remain, proving (5.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "[Component calculation, original Section 5.3.] On the open \\(a_1\\cdots a_d\\ne0\\), let \\( F(a,N_0,L_0)=\\sum_Ia^If_I(N_0,L_0) \\) be an irreducible factor arising from a bihomogeneous cut, of degrees \\((l,s_0)\\) in \\(a\\) …", "statement": "<p>[Component calculation, original Section 5.3.]</p><p>On the open \\(a_1\\cdots a_d\\ne0\\), let\n\\[\nF(a,N_0,L_0)=\\sum_Ia^If_I(N_0,L_0)\n\\]\nbe an irreducible factor arising from a bihomogeneous cut, of degrees \\((l,s_0)\\) in \\(a\\) and \\((N_0,L_0)\\). Omit factors \\(a_i\\) and \\(L_0\\), which do not meet the counted open. Put\n\\[\n\\nu_{i,I}=\\operatorname{ord}_{N_0-\\theta_iL_0}f_I,\n\\qquad\ne_i=\\min_I(I_i+k\\nu_{i,I}),\n\\]\nand write \\(f_I=\\prod_i(N_0-\\theta_iL_0)^{\\nu_{i,I}}g_I\\). Then\n\\[\nC(a,b)=\\sum_Ia^{I+k\\nu_I-e}b^{\\nu_I}g_I(N_0,L_0) \\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nis polynomial, has bidegree\n\\[\n(\\lambda,s_0),\\qquad\n\\lambda=l+ks_0-\\sum_ie_i,\n\\]\nand extends the open equation without an additional top-dimensional boundary component. Adding \\(C\\) to the bicone equations gives a prime complete intersection. Its leading bihomogeneous Hilbert term is\n\\[\n(\\lambda\\partial_A+s_0\\partial_B)R(A,B). \\tag{5.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every exponent of \\(a\\) in (5.6) is nonnegative, and on the bicone it represents \\(F/\\prod_i a_i^{e_i}\\). The two scaling actions preserve irreducible factors up to scalar, giving the asserted homogeneity.</p><p>On \\(a_i=0\\) with nonzero product vector, set \\(L_0=1\\). Substituting the other \\(b_j\\) leaves, exactly for \\(I_i+k\\nu_{i,I}=e_i\\), nonzero constants times\n\\[\na_{-i}^{I_{-i}-e_{-i}}b_i^{\\nu_{i,I}}.\n\\]\nThese Laurent monomials are distinct: their exponents determine \\(I_{-i},\\nu_{i,I}\\), then \\(I_i=e_i-k\\nu_{i,I}\\). Thus \\(C\\) is nonzero on the stratum and reduces its dimension to at most \\(d\\). The zero-product boundary already has dimension \\(d\\).</p><p>The open defined by \\(F\\) is integral and generically reduced of dimension \\(d+1\\). Since \\(C\\) is nonzero in the prime bicone ring, it preserves the complete-intersection property. The boundary count and the criterion of Lemma 4.1 prove primeness. The Hilbert series acquires \\(1-z_1^\\lambda z_2^{s_0}\\), giving (5.7), with error \\(O(A^{d-2})\\) at fixed \\(B/A\\). The two nonnegative degrees are not both zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "lattice coordinates and quartic exceptional fibers", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In paired maximal-coordinate boxes, assume \\( |x_i|\\ll X^{\\eta_+/d},\\qquad |y_i|\\ll X^{b_+/d}. \\) Fix \\(w,\\tau>0\\), and put \\( m=(d-1)w<\\min(\\eta_+,b_+), \\qquad u=(\\eta_+-m)/d,\\quad v=(b_+-m)/d. \\) Use a prime …", "statement": "<p>In paired maximal-coordinate boxes, assume\n\\[\n|x_i|\\ll X^{\\eta_+/d},\\qquad |y_i|\\ll X^{b_+/d}.\n\\]\nFix \\(w,\\tau&gt;0\\), and put\n\\[\nm=(d-1)w&lt;\\min(\\eta_+,b_+),\n\\qquad\nu=(\\eta_+-m)/d,\\quad v=(b_+-m)/d. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nUse a prime \\(M\\asymp X^w\\) for the direction grid.</p><p>The paired boxes can be grouped into boundedly many ranges indexed by \\(j=\\ell\\tau\\), \\(\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\), with \\(j\\le m/d\\). A range contains\n\\[\nO(X^{m-dj}) \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\npairs. In each pair there are integral unimodular changes \\(x\\leftrightarrow x'\\), \\(y\\leftrightarrow y'\\) such that\n\\[\n|x_i'|\\ll X^U,\\qquad |y_i'|\\ll X^V,\n\\qquad\nU=u+j+\\tau,\\quad V=v+j+\\tau. \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nThe matrices and their inverses have polynomially bounded entries.</p><p>In addition, if \\(d=4\\), an initial bounded-degree set \\(Z\\) from Proposition 4, restricted to \\(a_1a_2a_3a_4\\ne0\\), has an infinite-\\(n\\)-fiber base of dimension at most two and bounded degree, provided either \\(\\dim Z\\le3\\), or \\(Z\\) has an irreducible equation \\(F(a,n)=0\\) of positive \\(n\\)-degree. Deleting the selected points above this base costs \\(O_f(X^{2U})\\) per box. On every irreducible subvariety meeting the remaining selected set, \\(n\\) is algebraic over the field generated by the \\(a_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For an \\(s\\)-box with grid base \\(c_i/M\\), \\(|c_i|\\ll M\\), consider\n\\[\n\\Lambda_c=X^{-\\eta_+/d}\n\\{(q_0,Mq_1-c_1q_0,\\ldots,Mq_{d-1}-c_{d-1}q_0):q\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\}.\n\\]\nEvery counted \\(x\\) maps to a bounded vector because\n\\[\nMx_i-c_ix_0=Mx_0(s_i-c_i/M),\\qquad |s_i-c_i/M|\\le M^{-1}.\n\\]\nLet \\(U_*\\) be the inverse shortest length of \\(\\Lambda_c\\). Lemma 5.1 gives a unimodular coordinate change with \\(|x_i'|\\ll U_*\\). Construct the corresponding \\(y\\)-lattice, scaled by \\(X^{-b_+/d}\\), and let \\(V_*\\) be its inverse shortest length.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nJ=\\max\\{1,U_*/X^u,V_*/X^v\\}.\n\\]\nBefore scaling, all nonzero lattice vectors are nonzero integer vectors. Hence\n\\[\nU_*\\le X^{\\eta_+/d},\\quad V_*\\le X^{b_+/d},\n\\quad 1\\le J\\le X^{m/d}. \\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nThe range \\(X^j\\le J&lt;X^{j+\\tau}\\) gives (6.3).</p><p>For \\(j&gt;0\\), suppose \\(U_*\\ge X^{u+j}\\). A shortest vector gives a nonzero integer residual vector\n\\[\nq=(q_0,Mq_1'-c_1q_0,\\ldots,Mq_{d-1}'-c_{d-1}q_0),\n\\qquad\n\\|q\\|\\le T:=X^{m/d-j}.\n\\]\nHere \\(1\\le T\\le X^{m/d}\\), and \\(T/M\\ll X^{-w/d}\\), so \\(T&lt;M\\) for large \\(X\\). If \\(q_0=0\\), every coordinate is divisible by \\(M\\), impossible. Thus \\(0&lt;|q_0|&lt;M\\), and primality gives\n\\[\nc_i\\equiv-q_iq_0^{-1}\\pmod M.\n\\]\nEach residual vector determines boundedly many grid bases; there are \\(O(T^d)=O(X^{m-dj})\\) vectors. The \\(V_*\\)-case is identical. Proposition 3 gives boundedly many partner boxes in either direction. For \\(j=0\\), use the total \\(O(X^m)\\) bound.</p><p>The covolume is \\(X^{-\\eta_+}M^{d-1}\\asymp X^{m-\\eta_+}\\), and the shortest length is at least \\(X^{-\\eta_+/d}\\). Lemma 5.1 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\max_i\\|b_i\\|\\ll X^{m-\\eta_+/d}.\n\\]\nThe inverse unscaled triangular map has bounded norm, so the basis pulls back to integer vectors of size \\(O(X^m)\\). Cofactors bound the inverse unimodular matrix polynomially; likewise for \\(y\\).</p><p>Blockwise linear changes preserve degrees, dimensions, and weighted polynomial filtrations when every coordinate in a block has the same weight. The quartic assertions are Lemma 5.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\Lambda\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d\\) be a full lattice of covolume \\(\\Delta\\), and let \\(\\lambda_1\\) be its shortest nonzero Euclidean length.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\Lambda\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d\\) be a full lattice of covolume \\(\\Delta\\), and let \\(\\lambda_1\\) be its shortest nonzero Euclidean length. There is a lattice basis \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_d\\) with\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^d\\|b_i\\|\\le C_d\\Delta.\n\\]\nIn this basis, every \\(v\\in\\Lambda\\) with \\(\\|v\\|\\le C\\) has integer coordinates of absolute value at most \\(CC_d/\\lambda_1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(d\\). Choose a shortest primitive \\(b_1\\) and project orthogonally onto \\(b_1^\\perp\\). The projected lattice has covolume \\(\\Delta/\\|b_1\\|\\) and kernel \\(\\mathbb Zb_1\\). A nonzero projected vector has length at least \\(\\sqrt3\\|b_1\\|/2\\): lift it and reduce its parallel component to length at most \\(\\|b_1\\|/2\\), while the resulting lattice vector has length at least \\(\\|b_1\\|\\).</p><p>Apply induction to the projected lattice and lift its basis with reduced parallel components. Each lift has length at most \\(2/\\sqrt3\\) times its projected length. Together with \\(b_1\\), these form a basis and prove the product bound.</p><p>Cramer's rule gives\n\\[\n|z_i|\\le\\frac{C\\prod_{h\\ne i}\\|b_h\\|}{\\Delta}\n\\le\\frac{CC_d}{\\|b_i\\|}\n\\le\\frac{CC_d}{\\lambda_1}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the quartic assumptions in the last paragraph of Proposition 5, write \\( b_i=(n-\\theta_i)/a_i^2. \\) The closure of the set of \\(a\\) for which the \\(n\\)-fiber in \\(Z\\) has dimension one has dimension at most two …", "statement": "<p>Under the quartic assumptions in the last paragraph of Proposition 5, write\n\\[\nb_i=(n-\\theta_i)/a_i^2.\n\\]\nThe closure of the set of \\(a\\) for which the \\(n\\)-fiber in \\(Z\\) has dimension one has dimension at most two and bounded degree. The stated deletion cost and algebraicity conclusion follow uniformly under the blockwise coordinate changes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Substitute \\(b_i=(n-\\theta_i)/a_i^2\\) in bounded-degree equations for \\(Z\\) and clear denominators. A fiber has dimension one precisely when every resulting polynomial in \\(n\\) vanishes identically. Its base is therefore cut out by bounded-degree coefficient polynomials in \\(a\\); retain the relevant components in the \\(a\\)-torus and take closure.</p><p>If \\(\\dim Z\\le3\\), the fiber-dimension inequality bounds the base dimension by two. If \\(Z\\) has irreducible \\(F(a,n)\\) of positive \\(n\\)-degree, its coefficients have no common nonconstant factor, by Proposition 4. A codimension-one common component would divide them all, so their common zero set has codimension at least two.</p><p>An invertible linear change to \\(x'\\) preserves dimension and degree. A bounded-degree set of dimension at most two has \\(O(B^2)\\) integer points in a box of radius \\(B\\), independently of coefficients: slice each positive-dimensional irreducible component by a nonconstant coordinate and induct, ending with the degree bound for finite sets. Take \\(B\\asymp X^U\\). Each \\(x'\\) costs \\(O_f(1)\\) selected points by Proposition 3.</p><p>Finally, if \\(n\\) were transcendental over the \\(a\\)-field on an irreducible \\(Y\\subseteq Z\\), every substituted relation in \\(n\\) would have all coefficients zero on \\(Y\\). Its \\(a\\)-image would lie in the removed base, so \\(Y\\) would meet no surviving selected point.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 7.1 and its dimensional consequences", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Give each \\(a_i\\) weight \\(U>0\\) and each \\(b_i\\) weight \\(V>0\\), with \\(U,V\\) rational.", "statement": "<p>Give each \\(a_i\\) weight \\(U&gt;0\\) and each \\(b_i\\) weight \\(V&gt;0\\), with \\(U,V\\) rational. Let \\(Y\\) be irreducible of dimension \\(2\\le h\\le d\\) in\n\\[\na_i^kb_i=n-\\theta_i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le d),\n\\]\nmeeting \\(a_1\\cdots a_d\\ne0\\), and suppose \\(n\\) is nonconstant.</p><p>Choose \\(h-1\\) of the \\(a_i\\) algebraically independent over \\(\\mathbb C(n)\\), and one more distinct index. Relabel these \\(1,\\ldots,h\\). The image in their \\(a\\)-coordinates and \\(n\\) is an irreducible hypersurface with equation\n\\[\nF_1(a_1,\\ldots,a_h,n)=\\sum_Ia^If_I(n),\n\\]\nnot divisible by any \\(a_i\\). Let \\(Y_{\\rm pair}\\) be the closure of its image in the selected pairs \\((a_i,b_i)_{i=1}^h\\). Define\n\\[\n\\nu_{i,I}=\\operatorname{ord}_{n-\\theta_i}f_I,\\qquad\ne_i=\\min_I(I_i+k\\nu_{i,I}),\n\\]\n\\[\nD=\\max_I\\{U|I|+(kU+V)\\deg f_I\\}-U\\sum_ie_i. \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nThen \\(D&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\nH_{Y_{\\rm pair}}(T)\n=\\frac{(kU+V)^{h-1}D}{U^hV^h}\\frac{T^h}{h!}\n+O(T^{h-1}+1). \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nThis is a lower bound for \\(H_Y(T)\\). If\n\\[\n[\\mathbb C(Y):\\mathbb C(Y_{\\rm pair})]\\ge2,\n\\]\nthe leading coefficient in the lower bound can be doubled. For fixed weights and bounded degree of \\(Y\\), all errors are uniform in equation coefficients and blockwise coordinate changes.</p><p>For the initial sets of Proposition 4, the resulting lower-bound coefficients are:</p><p class=\"li\">in dimension \\(d\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{(kU+V)^{d-1}(U+V)}{U^dV^d};\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">in dimensions \\(2\\le h\\le d-1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{(kU+V)^{h-1}\\min(U,V)}{U^hV^h};\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">whenever \\(h\\) algebraically independent \\(a\\)-coordinates can be selected,\n\\[\n\\frac{(kU+V)^{h-1}V}{U^hV^h}.\n\\]\nAfter the quartic deletion in Proposition 5, the last alternative is available on every remaining subvariety with nonconstant \\(n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The function field on the nonzero-\\(a\\) open is generated by the \\(a_i\\) and \\(n\\); the specified choice therefore gives the hypersurface image of dimension \\(h\\). Lemmas 6.1–6.2 prove (7.2).</p><p>The degree of \\(F_1\\) is bounded in terms of \\(d,k,\\deg Y\\): form the graph of the fixed-degree map \\(n=a_1^kb_1+\\theta_1\\) and project. Graph and projection degree bounds do not use coefficient heights. For fixed weights and bounded \\(\\deg F_1\\), only finitely many numerator degrees \\(E\\) occur in (7.7), so errors are uniform.</p><p>Pullback preserves weights and injects pair-image functions into functions on \\(Y\\). If the field extension has degree at least two, some original coordinate \\(\\xi\\) of weight \\(\\omega\\in\\{U,V\\}\\) lies outside the pair field. The spaces of pair functions and \\(\\xi\\) times pair functions are independent:\n\\[\nH_Y(T)\\ge\nH_{Y_{\\rm pair}}(T)+H_{Y_{\\rm pair}}(T-\\omega). \\tag{7.8}\n\\]\nThe bounded shift doubles the leading coefficient without changing the error order.</p><p>If \\(\\dim Y=d\\), it is a mixed component from Proposition 4. Homogeneity and exact \\(n\\)-degree give\n\\[\nD=lU+(kU+V)s_0-U\\sum_ie_i\n=\\lambda U+s_0V\\ge U+V.\n\\]\nFor smaller dimensions use \\(D\\ge\\min(U,V)\\).</p><p>If the chosen \\(h\\) \\(a\\)-coordinates are algebraically independent, \\(C_1\\) must involve a \\(b\\)-coordinate; otherwise it is a nonzero relation among those \\(a\\)'s. Hence \\(D\\ge V\\). After Proposition 5's quartic deletion, \\(n\\) is algebraic over the \\(a\\)-field, which has transcendence degree \\(h\\), so this independent selection exists.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "extracting the pair equation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 6, write \\( f_I(n)=\\prod_{i=1}^h(n-\\theta_i)^{\\nu_{i,I}}g_I(n), \\) and define \\( C_1(a,b)= \\sum_Ia^{I+k\\nu_I-e}b^{\\nu_I}g_I(a_1^kb_1+\\theta_1). \\) Together with …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 6, write\n\\[\nf_I(n)=\\prod_{i=1}^h(n-\\theta_i)^{\\nu_{i,I}}g_I(n),\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nC_1(a,b)=\n\\sum_Ia^{I+k\\nu_I-e}b^{\\nu_I}g_I(a_1^kb_1+\\theta_1). \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nTogether with\n\\[\na_i^kb_i+\\theta_i=a_1^kb_1+\\theta_1\n\\qquad(2\\le i\\le h), \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nthis agrees with the pair image on the nonzero-\\(a\\) open. Its affine boundary has dimension at most \\(h-1\\). The polynomial \\(C_1\\) has exact weight \\(D\\), and\n\\[\nD\\ge\\min(U,V)&gt;0. \\tag{7.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The exponents of \\(a\\) are nonnegative. On the nonzero-\\(a\\) open, the ring is\n\\[\n\\mathbb C[a_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,a_h^{\\pm1},n]/(F_1),\n\\]\nan integral ring of dimension \\(h\\).</p><p>If \\(a_i=0\\), then \\(n=\\theta_i\\), every other \\(a_j\\ne0\\), and\n\\[\nb_j=(\\theta_i-\\theta_j)/a_j^k.\n\\]\nBefore \\(C_1\\), the free coordinates \\(b_i,a_{-i}\\) give dimension \\(h\\). Surviving terms of \\(C_1\\), with \\(I_i+k\\nu_{i,I}=e_i\\), are distinct nonzero multiples of\n\\[\na_{-i}^{I_{-i}-e_{-i}}b_i^{\\nu_{i,I}}.\n\\]\nThey cannot cancel, so the boundary dimension drops to at most \\(h-1\\). Two distinct \\(a_i\\) cannot vanish because the \\(\\theta_i\\) are distinct.</p><p>Every term has weight at most \\(D\\). In the highest-weight parts of (7.4), write the common product as \\(q\\). On their nonzero-\\(a\\) open, the weight-\\(D\\) part of \\(C_1\\) restricts to\n\\[\na^{-e}\\operatorname{in}_{(U,kU+V)}F_1(a,q). \\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nThis is a nonzero Laurent polynomial: distinct \\(a^Iq^j\\) remain distinct after division by \\(a^e\\). Thus the exact weight is \\(D\\). The polynomial is nonconstant, since otherwise its nonempty open hypersurface would be empty, proving (7.6).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "complete intersection at weighted infinity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(L_{\\rm wt}>0\\) such that \\( A=L_{\\rm wt}U,\\quad B=L_{\\rm wt}V \\) are integers, and put \\( Q=kA+B,\\qquad E=L_{\\rm wt}D. \\) Homogenize (7.3)–(7.4) with a variable \\(t\\) of weight one.", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(L_{\\rm wt}&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nA=L_{\\rm wt}U,\\quad B=L_{\\rm wt}V\n\\]\nare integers, and put\n\\[\nQ=kA+B,\\qquad E=L_{\\rm wt}D.\n\\]\nHomogenize (7.3)–(7.4) with a variable \\(t\\) of weight one. Their homogeneous ideal is prime and saturated with respect to \\(t\\). The Hilbert series of the filtered pair-image dimensions in the scaled weights is\n\\[\n\\frac{(1-z^Q)^{h-1}(1-z^E)}\n{(1-z)(1-z^A)^h(1-z^B)^h}. \\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nIts coefficient asymptotic, rescaled, is (7.2).</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(t\\ne0\\), scaling to \\(t=1\\) gives the affine scheme times \\(\\mathbb C^\\times\\). Its nonzero-\\(a\\) open is integral, generically reduced, and of dimension \\(h+1\\); its other affine strata have dimension at most \\(h\\).</p><p>At \\(t=0\\), all \\(a_i^kb_i\\) coincide. If all \\(a_i\\ne0\\), the coordinates \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_h,q\\) are free before the last equation, and (7.5) is nonzero, leaving dimension at most \\(h\\). If some \\(a_i=0\\), the common product is zero, so the locus is a union of coordinate \\(h\\)-planes. It has dimension \\(h\\), even if the last equation vanishes on such a plane.</p><p>Thus no top-dimensional component lies in a boundary. The complete-intersection criterion of Lemma 4.1 proves primeness and saturation. Dehomogenization gives the prime ideal of \\(Y_{\\rm pair}\\), since the open descriptions agree and boundaries are lower-dimensional.</p><p>Homogenizing each monomial proves surjectivity from the degree-\\(N\\) quotient to affine functions of scaled weight at most \\(N\\). Saturation proves injectivity. The regular-sequence Hilbert series is (7.7).</p><p>At \\(z=1\\), it has pole order \\(h+1\\) with leading factor \\(Q^{h-1}E/(A^hB^h)\\). At a nontrivial root of unity where only one of \\(1-z^A,1-z^B\\) vanishes, the pole order is at most \\(h\\). If both vanish, its order divides \\(\\gcd(A,B)\\), hence \\(Q\\) and \\(E\\), the latter being a monomial weight of \\(C_1\\). All \\(h\\) numerator factors vanish, again leaving order at most \\(h\\). Partial fractions give\n\\[\n\\frac{Q^{h-1}E}{A^hB^h}\\frac{N^h}{h!}+O(N^{h-1}+1).\n\\]\nTake \\(N=\\lfloor L_{\\rm wt}T\\rfloor\\) and rescale.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "the additional quartic surface bound", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(d=4\\), \\(k=2\\), and let \\(Y\\) be a geometrically irreducible surface defined over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) in the rational coordinates of the arithmetic boxes.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(d=4\\), \\(k=2\\), and let \\(Y\\) be a geometrically irreducible surface defined over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) in the rational coordinates of the arithmetic boxes. Suppose it meets \\(a_1a_2a_3a_4\\ne0\\), satisfies\n\\[\na_i^2b_i=n-\\theta_i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le4),\n\\]\nand \\(n\\) is nonconstant and algebraic over the field generated by \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_4\\). Give the two blocks positive rational weights \\(U,V\\), and set\n\\[\nD_0=\\min(3U,2V,U+V).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nH_Y(T)\\ge\n\\frac{T^2}{2}\n\\min\\left\\{\n\\frac{(2U+V)D_0}{U^2V^2},\\frac3{U^2}\n\\right\\}\n-O(T). \\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nFor fixed weights and bounded \\(\\deg Y\\), the error is uniform in moving coordinate changes and equations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(S\\) be the closure of the full \\(a\\)-image. It is a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined surface, since \\(n\\) is algebraic over its function field and \\(b_i=(n-\\theta_i)/a_i^2\\).</p><p>For an independent pair \\(a_i,a_j\\), Proposition 6 supplies a polynomial \\(C_1\\) of weight \\(D\\), involving a \\(b\\)-coordinate. If \\(D&lt;D_0\\), its monomials cannot contain two \\(b\\)'s, an \\(a\\) and a \\(b\\), or three \\(a\\)'s. Thus\n\\[\nc_ib_i+c_jb_j=S_{ij}(a_i,a_j),\n\\quad \\deg S_{ij}\\le2,\\quad(c_i,c_j)\\ne(0,0). \\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(D\\ge V\\). Substitution gives\n\\[\nB_{ij}n=c_i\\theta_i a_j^2+c_j\\theta_j a_i^2\n+a_i^2a_j^2S_{ij}(a_i,a_j),\n\\qquad\nB_{ij}=c_ia_j^2+c_ja_i^2. \\tag{8.3}\n\\]\nIndependence implies \\(B_{ij}\\ne0\\), so \\(n,b_i,b_j\\in\\mathbb C(a_i,a_j)\\).</p><p>If \\(\\deg S=e\\ge3\\), a general linear projection to an affine plane, with coordinates \\(s,t\\) linear in the \\(a_i\\), has field degree \\(e\\). Choose its projective center at infinity disjoint from the closure; a general fiber is a degree-\\(e\\) complementary section without points at infinity. A further general linear form \\(r\\) separates the extension, so \\(1,r,r^2\\) are independent over \\(\\mathbb C(s,t)\\). Therefore\n\\[\nH_Y(T)\\ge\n\\sum_{j=0}^2\\#\\{(p,q)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^2:U(p+q+j)\\le T\\}\n=\\frac{3T^2}{2U^2}-O(T).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\deg S=2\\), the degree–codimension bound cited in the paper shows that \\(S\\) spans an affine three-space \\(A\\) and is a quadratic hypersurface there. Its rational direction hyperplane in \\(K\\) has equation\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{K/\\mathbb Q}(\\gamma\\alpha)=0\n\\quad(\\gamma\\in K\\setminus\\{0\\}).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\ell_i\\) are the linear parts of \\(a_i|_A\\), then\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^4\\sigma_i(\\gamma)\\ell_i=0,\n\\]\nwith all coefficients nonzero. In the projective direction plane, the four lines \\(\\ell_i=0\\) have no triple intersection. Their six pair intersections are distinct.</p><p>The top quadratic part \\(Q_2\\) of the equation of \\(S\\) cannot vanish at all six intersections: on each of the four lines it would have three zeros, forcing all four line equations to divide \\(Q_2\\). Choose a pair \\(i,j\\) with \\(Q_2(v)\\ne0\\) at its intersection direction \\(v\\). The projection \\((a_i,a_j)\\) has quadratic fibers on \\(S\\), hence degree two and independent coordinates. If \\(D\\ge D_0\\), Proposition 6 suffices. If \\(D&lt;D_0\\), (8.3) identifies the pair field with \\(\\mathbb C(a_i,a_j)\\), while the full field has degree at least two over it. Proposition 6 doubles the coefficient, and \\(2D\\ge2V\\ge D_0\\).</p><p>Finally suppose \\(S=A\\) is a rational affine plane. Every \\(a_i\\) is nonconstant: a nonzero rational direction is a nonzero element of \\(K\\), with nonzero value under every embedding. Join \\(i,j\\) when their linear parts are independent. This is a connected complete multipartite graph with no isolated vertex.</p><p>Assume every edge has \\(D&lt;D_0\\), so (8.2)–(8.3) hold. If \\(n\\) is polynomial on \\(A\\), (8.3) gives \\(\\deg n\\le4\\), since \\(B_{ij}\\) has degree two and the right side degree at most six. Every index occurs with nonzero coefficient in some relation: start with a nonzero coefficient and use Galois transitivity, which preserves \\(Y\\), independence, and the degree bound. At the generic point of \\(a_i=0\\), an independent \\(a_j\\) is a unit; polynomiality makes \\(b_j\\) regular and the relation makes \\(b_i\\) regular. Hence\n\\[\na_i^2\\mid n-\\theta_i\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb C[A]\n\\qquad(1\\le i\\le4).\n\\]\nThe four zero lines are distinct. Restrict to a general affine line. A polynomial of degree at most four would have four distinct critical points with four distinct critical values, impossible for its derivative of degree at most three; a zero derivative would make the values equal.</p><p>If \\(n=P/Q\\) is nonpolynomial in reduced form, both coefficients in every relation are nonzero, since otherwise \\(n\\) would be polynomial. Any fixed irreducible factor of \\(Q\\) divides every \\(B_{ij}\\). Each \\(B_{ij}\\) is the product of two distinct affine linear forms \\(a_j\\pm\\zeta a_i\\), with \\(\\zeta\\ne0\\). The denominator factor defines a line through the common zero of every edge pair, transverse to both zero lines. Connectivity forces all four zero lines through one point \\(O\\).</p><p>Center coordinates at \\(O\\), scale them by \\(t\\), and take the rational-function limit as \\(t\\to0\\) in (8.3). On the line of directions, every independent pair gives the same function\n\\[\nR=\n\\frac{c_i\\theta_i a_j^2+c_j\\theta_j a_i^2}\n{c_ia_j^2+c_ja_i^2}. \\tag{8.4}\n\\]\nIt is a nonconstant fractional linear transform of \\((a_i/a_j)^2\\), of degree two, ramified exactly at \\(a_i=0,a_j=0\\), with values \\(\\theta_i,\\theta_j\\). Since every index occurs in an edge, the same function would be ramified in four directions with four distinct values. Coincident directions contradict the values; four distinct ones contradict degree two.</p><p>Thus some independent pair has \\(D\\ge D_0\\), and Proposition 6 proves the first alternative coefficient. All auxiliary independent functions have bounded weighted degree, preserving the uniform \\(O(T)\\) error.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "one global set of quintic values", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(d=4\\), with fixed ordered distinct roots \\(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_4\\) and splitting field \\(L\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(d=4\\), with fixed ordered distinct roots \\(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_4\\) and splitting field \\(L\\). There are finitely many \\(\\Phi\\in L[t]\\) of degree five with distinct critical points\n\\[\nz_1=0,\\quad z_2=1,\\quad z_3,\\quad z_4,\n\\qquad\n\\Phi(z_i)=\\theta_i.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal F_f\\) be this family, and put\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_f=\n\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:n=\\Phi(z)\n\\text{ for some }\\Phi\\in\\mathcal F_f,\\ z\\in L\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\#(\\mathcal E_f\\cap[-2X,2X])\\ll_fX^{1/5}. \\tag{8.5}\n\\]\nIf \\(N(s)\\in L[s]\\) has degree five and distinct critical points \\(\\tau_i\\in L\\) with \\(N(\\tau_i)=\\theta_i\\), every integer value \\(N(s)\\), \\(s\\in L\\), belongs to \\(\\mathcal E_f\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 gives a finite family over \\(\\mathbb C\\), hence a finite subset over \\(L\\). Sum Lemma 8.2 over that subset to obtain (8.5).</p><p>For \\(N\\) in the statement, put\n\\[\n\\Phi(t)=N(\\tau_1+(\\tau_2-\\tau_1)t).\n\\]\nIts normalized critical points and values have the required order, so \\(\\Phi\\in\\mathcal F_f\\). Every \\(s\\in L\\) gives normalized input \\((s-\\tau_1)/(\\tau_2-\\tau_1)\\in L\\) with the same value. No coefficient-height restriction is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are finitely many complex degree-five polynomials with the normalized ordered critical points and values in Proposition 8.", "statement": "<p>There are finitely many complex degree-five polynomials with the normalized ordered critical points and values in Proposition 8.</p>", "proof": "<p>Introduce the six coefficients of \\(\\Phi\\) and \\(z_3,z_4\\), impose\n\\[\n\\Phi(z_i)=\\theta_i,\\qquad \\Phi'(z_i)=0,\n\\]\nand require the leading coefficient and all differences \\(z_i-z_j\\) to be nonzero. This is a finite-type locus. Its critical points are simple because they are distinct and exhaust the roots of the quartic derivative.</p><p>If a reduced irreducible component had positive dimension, its function field \\(F/\\mathbb C\\) would admit a nonzero derivation \\(\\partial\\): differentiate a transcendence-basis member and extend through the finite separable extension. Put \\(Q=\\partial\\Phi\\), differentiating coefficients. The value equations give \\(Q(z_i)=0\\), so\n\\[\nQ=A\\Phi',\\qquad \\deg A\\le1.\n\\]\nDifferentiating the critical equations gives\n\\[\n0=Q'(z_i)+\\Phi''(z_i)\\partial z_i\n=\\Phi''(z_i)(A(z_i)+\\partial z_i).\n\\]\nSince \\(z_1=0,z_2=1\\) are fixed and \\(\\Phi''(z_i)\\ne0\\), one has \\(A(0)=A(1)=0\\), hence \\(A=0\\). Thus \\(\\partial\\) kills all coefficients and critical points, which generate \\(F\\), contradicting \\(\\partial\\ne0\\). The locus is zero-dimensional and finite.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each fixed \\(\\Phi\\in L[t]\\) of degree five, \\( \\#\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:|n|\\le2X,\\ n=\\Phi(z)\\text{ for some }z\\in L\\} \\ll_{\\Phi,L}X^{1/5}. \\)", "statement": "<p>For each fixed \\(\\Phi\\in L[t]\\) of degree five,\n\\[\n\\#\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:|n|\\le2X,\\ n=\\Phi(z)\\text{ for some }z\\in L\\}\n\\ll_{\\Phi,L}X^{1/5}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(c&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\nc\\Phi(t)=A_5t^5+\\cdots+A_0,\\qquad A_i\\in\\mathcal O_L,\\quad A_5\\ne0.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Phi(z)=n\\in\\mathbb Z\\), then \\(w=A_5z\\) satisfies\n\\[\nw^5+A_4w^4+A_3A_5w^3+A_2A_5^2w^2\n+A_1A_5^3w+(A_0-cn)A_5^4=0. \\tag{8.6}\n\\]\nIt is monic over \\(\\mathcal O_L\\), so \\(w\\in\\mathcal O_L\\), and \\(z\\) lies in the fixed fractional lattice \\(A_5^{-1}\\mathcal O_L\\).</p><p>For every embedding \\(\\sigma:L\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb C\\),\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\Phi)(\\sigma(z))=n.\n\\]\nThe leading terms of these fixed polynomials imply\n\\[\n\\max_\\sigma|\\sigma(z)|\\ll_{\\Phi,L}X^{1/5}.\n\\]\nIn a fixed \\(\\mathbb Z\\)-basis \\(e_j\\) of the fractional lattice, write \\(z=\\sum_ju_je_j\\); inversion of the embedding matrix gives \\(|u_j|\\ll X^{1/5}\\).</p><p>Expand \\(\\Phi(\\sum_ju_je_j)\\) in a rational basis of \\(L\\) beginning with \\(1\\), and set all other coordinates to zero. After base change to \\(\\mathbb C\\), this rational-value locus is the inverse image of the diagonal under\n\\[\n(w_\\sigma)_\\sigma\\mapsto(\\sigma(\\Phi)(w_\\sigma))_\\sigma.\n\\]\nIts ring is\n\\[\n\\mathbb C[v,(w_\\sigma)_\\sigma]/\n(\\sigma(\\Phi)(w_\\sigma)-v:\\sigma).\n\\]\nThe equations are monic in separate variables after dividing by their leading coefficients. The ring is finite free of rank \\(5^{[L:\\mathbb Q]}\\) over \\(\\mathbb C[v]\\), with basis \\(\\prod_\\sigma w_\\sigma^{e_\\sigma}\\), \\(0\\le e_\\sigma&lt;5\\). Thus the locus has dimension one and bounded degree.</p><p>A bounded-degree affine set of dimension at most one has \\(O(R)\\) integer points in a box of radius \\(R\\): choose a nonconstant coordinate on each curve component, slice at its \\(O(R)\\) integer values, and apply Bézout to each finite fiber. Apply this with \\(R\\ll X^{1/5}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "curve alternatives", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Retain the arithmetic equations \\( a_i^kb_i=n-\\theta_i,\\qquad k=d-2,\\quad4\\le d\\le8, \\) in integral coordinates \\(x',y'\\), with respective weights \\(U,V>0\\) and bounds \\( |x_i'|\\ll_fX^U,\\qquad |y_i'|\\ll_fX^V. \\) The …", "statement": "<p>Retain the arithmetic equations\n\\[\na_i^kb_i=n-\\theta_i,\\qquad k=d-2,\\quad4\\le d\\le8,\n\\]\nin integral coordinates \\(x',y'\\), with respective weights \\(U,V&gt;0\\) and bounds\n\\[\n|x_i'|\\ll_fX^U,\\qquad |y_i'|\\ll_fX^V.\n\\]\nThe conjugate forms are blockwise linear combinations. The rational polynomial\n\\[\nn=\\frac1d\\sum_{i=1}^d(a_i^kb_i+\\theta_i) \\tag{9.1}\n\\]\nhas degree at most \\(k+1\\) in rational coordinates.</p><p>Let \\(S\\) be a selected set with at most one point per \\(n\\) and \\(O_f(1)\\) points per fixed \\(x'\\). When \\(d=4\\), remove the global values \\(\\mathcal E_f\\) of Proposition 8. Put\n\\[\ng=(d-1)(k-1)+\\mathbf1_{d=4},\n\\qquad\n\\delta_1(U,V)=\\max\\{U/2,\\min(U,V/g)\\}.\n\\]\nFor every fixed degree bound \\(E\\) and geometrically irreducible \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined curve \\(C\\) of degree at most \\(E\\) in the arithmetic variety, either\n\\[\n|S\\cap C|\\ll_{f,E,\\varepsilon}X^\\varepsilon\n\\quad\\text{for every }\\varepsilon&gt;0,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nH_C(T)\\ge \\frac{T}{\\delta_1(U,V)}-O_{f,E}(1). \\tag{9.2}\n\\]\nConstants are uniform in integral coordinate changes and for \\(U,V\\) in a fixed compact subset of \\((0,\\infty)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(n\\) is constant, use the fixed-\\(n\\) bound. If the \\(a\\)-image is constant, \\(x'\\) is constant, and use its multiplicity bound.</p><p>If the \\(a\\)-image is nonlinear, a general linear projection to a line has degree at least two. Choose its center at infinity away from the image's points at infinity. Let \\(t\\) be the projected linear function of weight at most \\(U\\). If the \\(a\\)-image is a line, take a nonconstant \\(x'\\)-coordinate for \\(t\\); the same argument applies whenever the full field has degree at least two over that line. In either case, some ambient coordinate \\(q\\notin\\mathbb C(t)\\). Hence\n\\[\n1,t,t^2,\\ldots,\\qquad q,qt,qt^2,\\ldots\n\\]\nare independent, with \\(q\\) of weight at most \\(\\max(U,V)\\). Thus\n\\[\nH_C(T)\\ge2T/U-O(1), \\tag{9.3}\n\\]\nwhich suffices because \\(\\delta_1\\ge U/2\\).</p><p>It remains to treat a rational line image with degree-one field extension. Choose a nonconstant integral coordinate \\(s=x_j'\\). The rational image line gives\n\\[\n\\alpha=rs+q,\\qquad r,q\\in K,\\quad r\\ne0,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\na_i=r_is+q_i,\\qquad\nr_i=\\sigma_i(r)\\ne0,\\quad q_i=\\sigma_i(q),\\quad r_i,q_i\\in L. \\tag{9.4}\n\\]\nThe field is \\(\\mathbb C(s)\\). Each \\(y_i'\\), and \\(n\\) from (9.1), belongs to \\(\\mathbb Q(s)\\), by uniqueness of a coprime representation with monic denominator and \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-descent. Their rational degrees are bounded by projecting the bounded-degree curve to their graphs; for \\(n\\), use the fixed-degree map (9.1).</p><p>If a \\(y_i'\\) is nonpolynomial, apply Lemma 9.1 to its bounded integral input \\(s\\) and output \\(y_i'\\). Each \\(s\\) fixes \\(x'\\), costing \\(O_f(1)\\) points, so the small-count alternative follows.</p><p>Otherwise all \\(y_i'\\), hence all \\(b_i\\) and \\(n\\), are polynomials. Put \\(\\tau_i=-q_i/r_i\\). Then\n\\[\n(s-\\tau_i)^k\\mid n(s)-\\theta_i.\n\\]\nThe \\(\\tau_i\\) are distinct, for equality would make distinct \\(\\theta_i\\) equal. Since \\(n\\) is nonconstant,\n\\[\n\\deg n\\ge1+d(k-1),\n\\qquad\n\\deg b_i=\\deg n-k\\ge(d-1)(k-1). \\tag{9.5}\n\\]\nIndeed \\(\\prod_i(s-\\tau_i)^{k-1}\\mid n'(s)\\).</p><p>For \\(d=4\\), equality \\(\\deg n=5\\) would give exactly four simple critical points with values \\(\\theta_i\\). The normalized polynomial\n\\[\n\\Phi(z)=n(\\tau_1+(\\tau_2-\\tau_1)z)\n\\]\nlies in \\(\\mathcal F_f\\). At a selected integral \\(s\\), its normalized input lies in \\(L\\), so \\(n\\in\\mathcal E_f\\). Such curves have no surviving points. Hence every remaining polynomial line curve has \\(\\deg b_i\\ge g\\).</p><p>Choose one \\(b_i\\) of degree \\(\\ell\\ge g\\). The functions\n\\[\ns^jb_i^a,\\qquad\n0\\le j&lt;g,\\quad a\\ge0,\\quad jU+aV\\le T,\n\\]\nhave distinct polynomial degrees \\(j+\\ell a\\), and therefore are independent. They give\n\\[\nH_C(T)\\ge gT/V-O_g(1+U/V).\n\\]\nPowers of \\(s\\) also give \\(T/U-O(1)\\). Their combination yields the bound with denominator \\(\\min(U,V/g)\\), and (9.3) gives (9.2). Uniform degree and weight bounds give all asserted uniformity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "integral values of a rational function", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix a positive integer \\(D\\) and positive constants \\(A,B,C_0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a positive integer \\(D\\) and positive constants \\(A,B,C_0\\). For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), \\(X\\ge2\\), and nonpolynomial \\(R\\in\\mathbb Q(s)\\) whose reduced numerator and denominator have degrees at most \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\{s\\in\\mathbb Z:|s|\\le C_0X^A,\\ R(s)\\in\\mathbb Z,\\\n|R(s)|\\le C_0X^B\\}\n\\ll_{D,A,B,C_0,\\varepsilon}X^\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nPoles are excluded, and the constant is independent of the coefficients of \\(R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(R=A_0/B_0\\) in lowest terms, with true degrees \\(a,b\\le D\\), \\(b\\ge1\\). If there are at most \\(a+b\\) counted inputs, there is nothing to prove. Otherwise select \\(a+b+1\\) distinct pairs \\((s_i,v_i)\\) and impose\n\\[\nA_1(s_i)-v_iB_1(s_i)=0,\\qquad\n\\deg A_1\\le a,\\quad\\deg B_1\\le b.\n\\]\nThe solution space is one-dimensional. For any solution,\n\\(A_1B_0-A_0B_1\\) has degree at most \\(a+b\\) and vanishes at all selected inputs, so it is zero. Coprimality and the true degree bounds force a scalar multiple of \\((A_0,B_0)\\).</p><p>The integer coefficient matrix has polynomially bounded entries in \\(X\\). Its maximal signed minors give a nonzero integer solution with coefficients \\(X^{O_{D,A,B,C_0}(1)}\\). Thus\n\\[\nR_0=\\operatorname{Res}(A_1,B_1)\n\\]\nis a nonzero integer of polynomial size. At every counted input, \\(B_1(s)\\ne0\\) and divides \\(A_1(s)\\), so the integral Bézout identity gives \\(B_1(s)\\mid R_0\\). The divisor bound gives \\(O_\\varepsilon(X^\\varepsilon)\\) signed possibilities for \\(B_1(s)\\), after adjusting the exponent. Each equation \\(B_1(s)=q\\) has at most \\(b\\) roots.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 10.1, with the coverage and exponent margins of Section 10.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(4\\le d\\le8\\), \\(k=d-2\\), and set \\( K_0=3000,\\qquad G_0=100000,\\qquad \\tau=1/50000. \\) Use \\(H,H_a,H_b\\) from Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(4\\le d\\le8\\), \\(k=d-2\\), and set\n\\[\nK_0=3000,\\qquad G_0=100000,\\qquad \\tau=1/50000.\n\\]\nUse \\(H,H_a,H_b\\) from Proposition 4. For integers \\(z\\ge K_0\\), put\n\\[\n\\eta_-=\\frac z{K_0},\\quad\n\\eta_+=\\frac{z+1}{K_0},\\quad\nb_-=d-k\\eta_+,\\quad b_+=d-k\\eta_-. \\tag{10.1}\n\\]\nStop at the first \\(z_d\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\eta_-(1-k\\eta_-/d)&lt;2495/10000. \\tag{10.2}\n\\]\nMode \\(Q\\) means \\(d=4\\) and \\(z&lt;4000\\).</p><p>For \\(U,V,D&gt;0\\), \\(h\\ge2\\), define\n\\[\n\\Psi_h(U,V;D)=\n\\frac{UV}{((kU+V)^{h-1}D)^{1/h}},\n\\qquad\ng=(d-1)(k-1)+\\mathbf1_{d=4}.\n\\]\nWith mode fixed, define\n\\[\n\\delta_d=\\Psi_d(U,V;U+V),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta_h=\\Psi_h(U,V;\\min(U,V))\n\\quad(2\\le h\\le d-1,\\text{ outside mode }Q),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta_3=\\Psi_3(U,V;V)\\quad(\\text{mode }Q),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta_2=\n\\max\\{\\Psi_2(U,V;\\min(3U,2V,U+V)),\\,U/\\sqrt3\\}\n\\quad(\\text{mode }Q),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta_1=\\max\\{U/2,\\min(U,V/g)\\},\n\\qquad\nE_h(U,V)=\\max_{h\\le s\\le d}\\delta_s(U,V). \\tag{10.3}\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(K_0\\le z&lt;z_d\\), positive rational \\(t_*,w\\), with \\(m=(d-1)w\\), can be chosen so that\n\\[\n0&lt;m&lt;1,\\qquad \\min(\\eta_-,b_-)&gt;m,\n\\]\nand both strict inequalities (5.2) hold with \\(\\eta_+,b_+\\). For\n\\[\nu=(\\eta_+-m)/d,\\qquad v=(b_+-m)/d,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n3/20\\le v/u\\le5,\\qquad\nm+\\sum_{h=1}^dE_h(u,v)&lt;999/1000,\n\\]\nand in mode \\(Q\\),\n\\[\nm+2u&lt;999/1000. \\tag{10.5}\n\\]\nThe stopping indices and conservative minimum gaps \\(1-m-\\sum_h\\overline E_h\\), before \\(d\\tau\\), are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|r|r|c|c}\nd&amp;z_d&amp;\\text{intervals}&amp;z_d/K_0&amp;\\text{gap}\\\\ \\hline\n4&amp;5124&amp;2124&amp;427/250&amp;191/100000\\\\\n5&amp;4084&amp;1084&amp;1021/750&amp;293/50000\\\\\n6&amp;3552&amp;552&amp;148/125&amp;321/2500\\\\\n7&amp;3226&amp;226&amp;1613/1500&amp;23177/100000\\\\\n8&amp;3003&amp;3&amp;1001/1000&amp;31683/100000\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nFor either mode, if \\(3/20\\le V/U\\le5\\), then\n\\[\n\\delta_h(U+s,V+s)\\le\\delta_h(U,V)+s\n\\quad(s\\ge0,\\ 1\\le h\\le d). \\tag{10.6}\n\\]</p><p>Consequently the lattice sums have exponents\n\\[\nm-dj+\\sum_hE_h(u+j+\\tau,v+j+\\tau)\n&lt;24979/25000&lt;1, \\tag{10.9}\n\\]\nand the quartic removed fibers have exponent\n\\[\nm-4j+2(u+j+\\tau)&lt;3122/3125&lt;1. \\tag{10.10}\n\\]\nThe remaining larger-\\(\\eta\\) range admits a fixed finite collection of positive rational upper weights \\(b',\\eta'\\) with\n\\[\n\\sqrt{b'\\eta'/d}&lt;4999/10000.\n\\]\nThis includes \\(b=0\\) and the fixed-coefficient \\(O_f(1/\\log X)\\) overshoot past \\(\\eta=d/k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 10.1–10.2 prove the finite choices, table, and shift estimate.</p><p>The transition function\n\\[\n\\phi_d(\\eta)=\\eta(1-k\\eta/d)\n\\]\nis nonincreasing for \\(\\eta\\ge1\\), since\n\\[\n\\phi_d'(\\eta)=1-2k\\eta/d\\le(4-d)/d\\le0.\n\\]\nThus the finite cells before the stopping index and the later range \\(\\phi_d(\\eta)&lt;2495/10000\\) meet without a gap. The last \\(Q\\)-cell ends at \\(4/3\\), where the ordinary thresholds begin.</p><p>For lattice weights \\(U=u+j+\\tau,V=v+j+\\tau\\), apply (10.6) to each threshold and then its cumulative maximum:\n\\[\nm-dj+\\sum_hE_h(U,V)\n\\le m+\\sum_hE_h(u,v)+d\\tau\n&lt;24979/25000.\n\\]\nFor mode \\(Q\\),\n\\[\nm-4j+2U=m+2u-2j+2\\tau&lt;3122/3125.\n\\]\nBoth leave room for the small loss in Proposition 2.</p><p>For the direct larger-\\(\\eta\\) range,\n\\[\n(4999/10000)^2-2495/10000\n=\\frac{40001}{100000000}&gt;0. \\tag{10.11}\n\\]\nLet \\(e=1/10000\\), and, for \\(1\\le\\eta\\le d/k\\), \\(b=d-k\\eta\\), take\n\\[\nb'=e(\\lfloor b/e\\rfloor+1),\\qquad\n\\eta'=e(\\lfloor\\eta/e\\rfloor+1).\n\\]\nThese are strict positive upper weights from a finite set. Since \\(b+\\eta\\le3\\) and \\(d\\ge4\\), when \\(b\\eta/d\\le2495/10000\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{b'\\eta'}d\n\\le\\frac{2495}{10000}+\\frac{3e+e^2}{4}\n&lt;(4999/10000)^2.\n\\]\nAlso include \\((b',\\eta')=(e,d/k+e)\\). It covers \\(d/k\\le\\eta&lt;d/k+e\\), including every fixed-coefficient \\(O_f(1/\\log X)\\) overshoot for large \\(X\\). Together with curve threshold \\(1/2\\), the direct counting exponent is below \\(9999/10000\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "[Exact parameter certificate, original Appendix A.] For \\( c_j=\\binom dj\\binom{d-2}{j-1}k^{j-1}, \\) one has …", "statement": "<p>[Exact parameter certificate, original Appendix A.]</p><p>For\n\\[\nc_j=\\binom dj\\binom{d-2}{j-1}k^{j-1},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nH(t)=\\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}c_jt^j,\\quad\nH_a(t)=\\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}\\frac{d-j}{d}c_jt^j,\\quad\nH_b(t)=\\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}\\frac jd c_jt^{j-1}. \\tag{10.7}\n\\]\nChoose \\(t_*\\) among \\(i/50\\), \\(1\\le i&lt;500\\), minimizing\n\\[\n\\frac{(\\eta_++b_+t)^d}{H(t)},\n\\]\nwith the first value breaking ties. Let \\(\\operatorname{up}_e(x)\\) be the least \\(r/G_0\\), \\(1\\le r\\le G_0\\), whose \\(e\\)-th power is strictly greater than \\(x\\). Then\n\\[\nw=\\operatorname{up}_{d-1}\\left(\n\\frac1{H(t_*)}\n\\left(\\frac{(d+1)(\\eta_++b_+t_*)}{d^2}\\right)^d\n\\right) \\tag{10.8}\n\\]\nsatisfies all finite assertions in Proposition 10. The endpoint derivative inequalities used in Lemma 10.2 also hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct differentiation proves (10.7). The binomial identities\n\\[\n(d-j)\\binom dj/d=\\binom{d-1}j,\\qquad\nj\\binom dj/d=\\binom{d-1}{j-1}\n\\]\ngive the three sums in the following original certificate. It uses only integers and rational numbers.</p><p>\\[\n\\text{The executable certificate is:}\n\\]\n“`python\nfrom fractions import Fraction as F\nfrom math import comb</p><p>K, G = 3000, 100000</p><p>def polynomials(d):\n    k = d - 2\n    for i in range(1, 500):\n        t = F(i, 50)\n        values = [sum(comb(d-r, j-s) * comb(k, j-1)</p><p class=\"li l2\">k**(j-1) * t**(j-s)\n                      for j in range(1, d))\n                  for r, s in [(0, 0), (1, 0), (1, 1)]]\n        yield (t, *values)</p><p>def upper(x, e):\n    assert 0 &lt;= x &lt; 1\n    l, r = 0, G\n    while r &gt; l + 1:\n        middle = (l + r) // 2\n        if F(middle, G)**e &gt; x:\n            r = middle\n        else:\n            l = middle\n    return F(r, G)</p><p>def check_derivatives():\n    for k in range(2, 7):\n        for t in [F(3, 20), F(5)]:\n            for a, b in [(1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1)]:\n                for h in range(2, k+3):\n                    N = t * (1 + 1/t</p><p class=\"li l2\">F(h-1, h)*(k+1)/(k+t)</p><p class=\"li l2\">F(1, h)*(a+b)/(a+b*t))\n                    assert N &gt;= 0\n                    assert N**h &lt; (k+t)**(h-1)*(a+b*t)</p><p>def check_degree(d):\n    k, choices, least = d-2, list(polynomials(d)), F(1)\n    for z in range(K, 2*K):\n        etaL, etaH = F(z, K), F(z+1, K)\n        if etaL*(1-k*etaL/d) &lt; F(2495, 10000):\n            return z, least\n        bL, bH = d-k*etaH, d-k*etaL\n        t, H, Ha, Hb = min(choices,\n            key=lambda row: (etaH+bH*row[0])**d/row[1])\n        w = upper(((d+1)*(etaH+bH*t)/d**2)**d/H, d-1)\n        assert (w**(d-2)*min(Ha, Hb)\n                &gt; ((etaH+bH*t)/(d-1))**(d-1))\n        m = (d-1)*w\n        u, v = (etaH-m)/d, (bH-m)/d\n        assert 0 &lt; m &lt; 1 and min(etaL, bL) &gt; m\n        assert min(u, v) &gt; 0 and F(3, 20) &lt;= v/u &lt;= 5</p><p>        def delta(h, D):\n            return upper((u*v)**h/((k*u+v)**(h-1)*D), h)</p><p>        P = [delta(d, u+v)]\n        if d == 4 and z &lt; 4000:\n            assert m+2*u &lt; F(999, 1000)\n            P += [delta(3, v),\n                  max(delta(2, min(3*u, 2*v, u+v)),\n                      upper(u*u/3, 2))]\n        else:\n            P += [delta(h, min(u, v))\n                  for h in range(d-1, 1, -1)]\n        g = (d-1)*(k-1) + (d == 4)\n        P.append(max(u/2, min(u, v/g)))</p><p>        gap = 1-m-sum(max(P[:h]) for h in range(1, d+1))\n        assert gap &gt; F(1, 1000)\n        least = min(least, gap)\n    raise AssertionError(\"cutoff not reached\")</p><p>check_derivatives()\nassert [check_degree(d) for d in range(4, 9)] == [\n    (5124, F(191, 100000)), (4084, F(293, 50000)),\n    (3552, F(321, 2500)), (3226, F(23177, 100000)),\n    (3003, F(31683, 100000))]\n“`\nThe function `upper` maintains\n\\[\n(l/G_0)^e\\le x&lt;(r/G_0)^e\n\\]\nand terminates at \\(r=l+1\\). Its output is a strict upper bound even when the root is already on the grid. The first strict cut inequality follows from its definition; all other finite conditions are explicitly checked.</p><p>The list `P` is ordered from dimension \\(d\\) down to \\(1\\), so its prefix maxima give conservative \\(E_d,\\ldots,E_1\\). Radical thresholds are rounded upward and therefore can only increase these maxima. Every interval, rather than sampled interpolation points, is checked; endpoint upper weights make each successful check valid throughout that interval.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the threshold functions in Proposition 10, the common-shift estimate (10.6) holds on \\(3/20\\le V/U\\le5\\).", "statement": "<p>For the threshold functions in Proposition 10, the common-shift estimate (10.6) holds on \\(3/20\\le V/U\\le5\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(a,b\\ge0\\), \\(a+b&gt;0\\), set\n\\[\nq_{a,b}(U,V)=\\frac{UV}{aU+bV},\n\\qquad\nF_{h,a,b}(U,V)=\n\\frac{UV}{((kU+V)^{h-1}(aU+bV))^{1/h}}.\n\\]\nThe Hessian of \\(q_{a,b}\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{-2ab}{(aU+bV)^3}\n\\begin{pmatrix}V^2&amp;-UV\\\\-UV&amp;U^2\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus \\(q_{a,b}\\) is concave and increasing; if one coefficient is zero it is a positive multiple of a coordinate. Since\n\\[\nF_{h,a,b}=q_{k,1}^{(h-1)/h}q_{a,b}^{1/h},\n\\]\nconcavity and monotonicity of the weighted geometric mean make \\(F_{h,a,b}\\) concave, increasing, and homogeneous of degree one.</p><p>Write \\(f(t)=F_{h,a,b}(1,t)\\). The \\((1,1)\\)-directional derivative is\n\\[\nD(t)=f(t)+(1-t)f'(t),\n\\qquad\nD'(t)=(1-t)f''(t).\n\\]\nIt is nonincreasing before \\(1\\) and nondecreasing after \\(1\\), so its maximum on \\([3/20,5]\\) occurs at an endpoint. Direct differentiation gives\n\\[\nD(t)=\n\\frac{N_{h,a,b}(t)}\n{((k+t)^{h-1}(a+bt))^{1/h}},\n\\]\n\\[\nN_{h,a,b}(t)=\nt\\left(1+\\frac1t-\\frac{h-1}{h}\\frac{k+1}{k+t}\n-\\frac1h\\frac{a+b}{a+bt}\\right).\n\\]\nLemma 10.1's `check_derivatives` verifies at both endpoints, for \\(2\\le k\\le6\\), \\(2\\le h\\le k+2\\), and \\((a,b)=(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)\\), that \\(N\\ge0\\) and\n\\[\nN^h&lt;(k+t)^{h-1}(a+bt).\n\\]\nThus \\(D(t)\\le1\\) throughout the interval.</p><p>The shifted ratio\n\\[\n\\frac{V+s}{U+s}\n=\\frac U{U+s}\\frac VU+\\frac s{U+s}\n\\]\nstays between \\(V/U\\) and \\(1\\). Integrate the derivative bound along this path. Multiplying the denominator form by \\(c\\ge1\\) multiplies the function by \\(c^{-1/h}\\le1\\), covering \\(3U,2V\\). Finite minima and maxima preserve the shift inequality. Finally \\(U/\\sqrt3,U/2,U,V/g\\) have directional slopes at most one, covering the remaining branches.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 1.4, proved in Section 11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be primitive and irreducible, with positive leading coefficient and degree \\(4\\le d\\le8\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be primitive and irreducible, with positive leading coefficient and degree \\(4\\le d\\le8\\). Put \\(k=d-2\\). There exists \\(\\delta=\\delta_f&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\#\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:X&lt;n\\le2X,\\ p^k\\mid f(n)\n\\text{ for some prime }p&gt;X\\}\n\\ll_fX^{1-\\delta}.\n\\]\nLocal admissibility is not required.</p>", "proof": "<p>For sufficiently large \\(X\\),\n\\[\n0&lt;f(n)\\ll_fX^d\\qquad(X&lt;n\\le2X),\n\\]\nso any relevant prime satisfies \\(p\\ll_fX^{d/k}\\). Split \\(p&gt;X\\) into \\(O_f(\\log X)\\) dyadic intervals, truncating the last. Then\n\\[\n1\\le\\eta\\le d/k+O_f(1/\\log X).\n\\]</p><p>Lemma 11.1 handles the direct surface range; Proposition 10's cells cover its complement, so Lemma 11.2 handles every other interval, including all boundaries. The determinant degrees, patch degrees, Hilbert errors, and tolerances were fixed using strict margins before \\(X\\) grows.</p><p>Take the minimum of the finitely many savings, increase the lower threshold for \\(X\\) to absorb bounded constants, and sum over \\(O_f(\\log X)\\) intervals. A smaller positive saving absorbs this logarithm. The single quartic exceptional set is also absorbed after decreasing the saving if necessary.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "direct surface range", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix a dyadic prime interval \\([P,2P]\\), select one qualifying prime for each qualifying input, and put \\( \\eta=\\frac{\\log P}{\\log X},\\qquad b=d-k\\eta. \\) If \\( \\eta(1-k\\eta/d)\\le2495/10000, \\) the number of selected …", "statement": "<p>Fix a dyadic prime interval \\([P,2P]\\), select one qualifying prime for each qualifying input, and put\n\\[\n\\eta=\\frac{\\log P}{\\log X},\\qquad b=d-k\\eta.\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\n\\eta(1-k\\eta/d)\\le2495/10000, \\tag{11.1}\n\\]\nthe number of selected triples has a positive power saving, uniform over these intervals for sufficiently large \\(X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The triples\n\\[\n(x,y,z)=\\left(n,\\frac{f(n)}{p^k},p\\right)\n\\]\nlie on\n\\[\nf(x)=yz^k. \\tag{11.2}\n\\]\nThis affine surface is irreducible over \\(\\mathbb C\\), by Gauss's lemma: it is primitive and linear in \\(y\\) over \\(\\mathbb C[x,z]\\), with coprime coefficients \\(z^k,f(x)\\). Bounds are \\(O_f(X),O_f(X^b),O_f(X^\\eta)\\).</p><p>Use the finite positive rational upper weights \\(b',\\eta'\\) of Proposition 10, satisfying\n\\[\n\\sqrt{b'\\eta'/d}&lt;0.4999. \\tag{11.3}\n\\]\nFor weights \\((1,b',\\eta')\\), the filtered Hilbert function is\n\\[\nH(T)=\\frac{D}{b'\\eta'}\\frac{T^2}{2}+O(T),\n\\qquad D=\\max\\{d,b'+k\\eta'\\}. \\tag{11.4}\n\\]\nIndeed multiplication by the defining polynomial increases weight exactly by \\(D\\). With integer-scaled weights \\(A=L,B=Lb',C=L\\eta',E=LD\\), weighted homogenization gives\n\\[\n\\frac{1-t^E}{(1-t)(1-t^A)(1-t^B)(1-t^C)}.\n\\]\nThe pole at one has order three and leading factor \\(E/(ABC)\\). Other roots have pole order at most two: if their orders divide all three coordinate weights, they divide the monomial weight \\(E\\), and the numerator cancels a factor. Coefficient extraction proves (11.4). Thus\n\\[\n\\delta_2=\\sqrt{b'\\eta'/D}&lt;0.4999.\n\\]</p><p>For a bounded-degree irreducible \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined curve on the surface, constant \\(x\\) contributes at most one selected point. If its field has degree at least two over \\(\\mathbb C(x)\\), some other coordinate lies outside that subfield, and two independent families of powers of \\(x\\) give \\(H(T)\\ge2T-O(1)\\).</p><p>Otherwise \\(y,z\\in\\mathbb Q(x)\\), of uniformly bounded degrees by projection. A nonpolynomial coordinate is covered by Lemma 9.1. If both are polynomials, separability of \\(f\\) in\n\\[\nf(x)=y(x)z(x)^k\n\\]\nforces \\(z\\) to be constant. At selected points it is a prime \\(p&gt;X\\). Outside fixed bad primes, \\(f\\) has at most \\(d\\) roots modulo \\(p^k&gt;X\\), so the curve contributes \\(O_f(1)\\) inputs.</p><p>The only two-dimensional irreducible subvariety is the surface. Proposition 2 therefore applies with \\(\\delta_1=1/2\\), giving\n\\[\nO_{f,\\varepsilon}(X^{\\delta_2+1/2+\\varepsilon}).\n\\]\nThe finite weight list permits one sufficiently small \\(\\varepsilon\\) and a common positive saving.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "number-field range", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a dyadic interval not satisfying (11.1), use the finite parameter cell, weights, thresholds, and lattice grouping of Proposition 10.", "statement": "<p>For a dyadic interval not satisfying (11.1), use the finite parameter cell, weights, thresholds, and lattice grouping of Proposition 10. The number of its selected inputs is \\(O_f(X^{1-\\delta'})\\) for a common \\(\\delta'&gt;0\\), after accounting for one global quartic exceptional set of size \\(O_f(X^{1/5})\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(d=4\\), remove once the values of Proposition 8, before the choices of prime intervals, boxes, or curves. Proposition 3 gives paired arithmetic boxes; the strict cell inequalities allow Proposition 4's cuts. Each pair gives boundedly many bounded-degree affine sets of dimension at most \\(d-1\\), except mixed components of dimension \\(d\\).</p><p>By Proposition 5, a lattice range \\(j\\) has\n\\[\nO_f(X^{m-dj})\\text{ pairs},\n\\qquad\n|x_i'|\\ll_fX^U,\\quad |y_i'|\\ll_fX^V,\n\\quad U=u+j+\\tau,\\quad V=v+j+\\tau. \\tag{11.5}\n\\]\nThere are boundedly many ranges.</p><p>In mode \\(Q\\), remove the infinite-fiber points from each initial set, at cost \\(O_f(X^{2U})\\). Propositions 6, 7, and 9 then give, for every bounded-degree irreducible \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined subvariety meeting the remaining set, either the uniform small-count alternative, or\n\\[\nH_A(T)\\ge\n\\frac{T^h}{h!\\delta_h(U,V)^h}-O(T^{h-1}),\n\\qquad h=\\dim A&gt;0.\n\\]\nOutside mode \\(Q\\), use the general bounds of Propositions 6 and 9. Constant \\(n\\) or constant first tuple has the established bounded count.</p><p>Proposition 2, in ambient dimension \\(2d\\) and dimension bound \\(d\\), gives per remaining set\n\\[\nO_{f,\\varepsilon}\\left(X^{\\sum_hE_h(U,V)+\\varepsilon}\\right).\n\\]\nIts hypotheses hold for every bounded degree, including the later moving cut varieties. Multiplying by the box count and using Proposition 10,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm-dj+\\sum_hE_h(u+j+\\tau,v+j+\\tau)+\\varepsilon\n&amp;\\le m+\\sum_hE_h(u,v)+d\\tau+\\varepsilon\\\\\n&amp;&lt;24979/25000+\\varepsilon.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nChoose a sufficiently small fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>The deleted quartic fibers, including the number of boxes, have exponent\n\\[\nm-4j+2(u+j+\\tau)&lt;3122/3125.\n\\]\nFinite cell, component, and lattice-range multiplicities affect only constants. All savings are fixed before \\(X\\to\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Factorwise tail estimate used in the proof of original Corollary 1.3.] Fix \\(k\\ge2\\).", "statement": "<p>[Factorwise tail estimate used in the proof of original Corollary 1.3.]</p><p>Fix \\(k\\ge2\\). Let \\(g\\in\\mathbb Z[x]\\) be primitive and irreducible with positive leading coefficient, degree \\(d\\le k+2\\), and no fixed prime \\(k\\)-th-power divisor. Define\n\\[\nT_{g,k}(X)=\n\\#\\{n\\in\\mathbb Z:X&lt;n\\le2X,\\ p^k\\mid g(n)\n\\text{ for some prime }p&gt;X\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nT_{g,k}(X)=o_{g,k}(X). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\nFinitely many integer roots, when present, may be omitted.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(d\\le k\\), use \\(|g(n)|\\ll_gX^d\\). If \\(d&lt;k\\), the tail is empty for large \\(X\\). If \\(d=k\\), only \\(X&lt;p\\le C_gX\\) occur. Outside finitely many primes there are at most \\(d\\) roots modulo \\(p^k\\), and an interval of length \\(X&lt;p^k\\) has at most one representative of each. Thus\n\\[\nT_{g,k}(X)\\ll_g\\pi(C_gX)=o_g(X).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(d=k+1\\), use the unweighted triple estimates in Reuss [21, Lemma 3 and its proof, and Section 6], as cited in the paper. They give \\(o_g(X)\\) triples\n\\[\nX&lt;n\\le2X,\\quad a&gt;X^{1-\\delta},\\quad \\mu^2(a)=1,\n\\quad a^{d-1}b=g(n)\n\\]\nfor a suitable fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\). For large \\(X\\), \\(g(n)&gt;0\\), and choosing \\(a=p\\), \\(b=g(n)/p^k\\), includes every tail input.</p><p>If \\(d=k+2\\le8\\), apply Proposition 11. If \\(d=k+2\\ge9\\), then\n\\[\nk=d-2\\ge(3d+1)/4,\\qquad k/d&gt;3/4.\n\\]\nXiao [27, Section 9], in the cited formulation of Browning's estimate, gives\n\\[\nE_B(B^{1-\\delta})=o_g(B),\n\\quad\nE_B(\\xi)=\n\\#\\{1\\le n\\le B:b^k\\mid g(n)\n\\text{ for some squarefree integer }b&gt;\\xi\\}.\n\\]\nTake \\(B=2X\\); eventually \\(p&gt;X&gt;(2X)^{1-\\delta}\\), so this exceptional set contains the tail.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Supplementary cyclotomic arithmetic, original Section 12.2, including Lemma 12.3.] Let \\(\\zeta=\\exp(2\\pi i/8)\\) and \\(R=\\mathbb Z[\\zeta]\\).", "statement": "<p>[Supplementary cyclotomic arithmetic, original Section 12.2, including Lemma 12.3.]</p><p>Let \\(\\zeta=\\exp(2\\pi i/8)\\) and \\(R=\\mathbb Z[\\zeta]\\). The ring is Euclidean for the absolute field norm.</p><p>If \\(p^2\\mid n^4+1\\), one may choose\n\\[\nA^2B=n-\\zeta,\\qquad A,B\\in R,\\qquad N(A)=p, \\tag{12.1}\n\\]\nwith primitive coefficient vectors in \\(1,\\zeta,\\zeta^2,\\zeta^3\\). For \\(N\\le n\\le2N\\), \\(P\\le p&lt;2P\\), the factors can be balanced so that\n\\[\n|\\sigma(A)|\\asymp P^{1/4},\n\\qquad\n|\\sigma(B)|\\asymp NP^{-1/2}\n\\]\nat every embedding.</p><p>Uniformly in positive integers \\(D\\), the number of positive integer pairs \\((n,q)\\) with\n\\[\nN\\le n\\le2N,\\qquad n^4+1=Dq^2\n\\]\nis \\(O(1+\\log N)\\). Consequently, for each fixed \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\{N\\le n\\le2N:p^2\\mid n^4+1\n\\text{ for some prime }p&gt;N^{3/2+\\epsilon}\\}\n=O(N^{1-2\\epsilon}(1+\\log N)).\n\\]</p><p>A fixed projective \\(B\\)-direction has at most two primitive integral representatives, fixes \\(D=N(B)\\), and gives the same Pell equation with \\(q=p\\). A fixed \\(A\\)-direction fixes \\(p=N(A)\\) and a residue class modulo \\(p^2\\), contributing \\(O(1+N/p^2)\\) inputs.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 13.1 gives the factorization. Lemma 13.2 gives the Pell count. If \\(p&gt;N^{3/2+\\epsilon}\\), then\n\\[\nD=(n^4+1)/p^2\\le17N^{1-2\\epsilon}.\n\\]\nSum the uniform Pell bound over these positive integers \\(D\\).</p><p>Primitive vectors in one rational projective direction differ only by sign, giving the \\(B\\)-direction assertion. Taking norms in (12.1) yields \\(n^4+1=N(B)p^2\\). The \\(A\\)-direction fixes \\(p\\), and evaluation modulo \\(p^2\\) fixes the stated input class.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The Euclidean and balanced factorization assertions of Proposition 13 hold.", "statement": "<p>The Euclidean and balanced factorization assertions of Proposition 13 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(R=\\mathbb Z[i]+\\zeta\\mathbb Z[i]\\). Given \\(a+b\\zeta\\in\\mathbb Q(\\zeta)\\), round \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Q(i)\\) to Gaussian integers, with errors \\(c,d\\) satisfying \\(|c|^2,|d|^2\\le1/2\\). Then\n\\[\n|N_{\\mathbb Q(\\zeta)/\\mathbb Q}(c+d\\zeta)|\n=|c^2-id^2|^2&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality is strict unless both squared moduli are \\(1/2\\). In that case all real and imaginary parts have absolute value \\(1/2\\), so \\(c^2,id^2\\) are perpendicular and the squared modulus is \\(1/2\\). Thus \\(R\\) is norm-Euclidean and every ideal is principal.</p><p>Evaluation \\(\\zeta\\mapsto n\\) gives surjections\n\\[\n\\varphi_j:R\\to\\mathbb Z/p^j\\mathbb Z,\\qquad j=1,2.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\ker\\varphi_1=(A)\\). Its index is \\(p\\), hence \\(N(A)=p\\), the norm being positive by complex conjugate pairs. The inclusion \\((A)^2\\subseteq\\ker\\varphi_2\\) is equality by indices, giving (12.1).</p><p>The unit \\(1+\\sqrt2\\), with \\(\\sqrt2=\\zeta-\\zeta^3\\), has absolute values \\(1+\\sqrt2\\) and \\((1+\\sqrt2)^{-1}\\) at the complex places. A suitable power balances \\(A\\); the identity balances \\(B\\). A common integer divisor of either coefficient vector would divide the coefficient \\(-1\\) of \\(\\zeta\\) in \\(A^2B\\), proving primitivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma 12.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The uniform Pell count in Proposition 13 is \\(O(1+\\log N)\\).", "statement": "<p>The uniform Pell count in Proposition 13 is \\(O(1+\\log N)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(x=n^2\\). If \\(D\\) is a square, factoring \\(Dq^2-x^2=1\\) rules out \\(x&gt;0\\). Otherwise attach\n\\[\n\\alpha=x+q\\sqrt D=x+\\sqrt{x^2+1},\n\\]\nof norm \\(-1\\). For distinct solutions with \\(\\alpha_2&gt;\\alpha_1\\),\n\\[\n\\gamma=\\alpha_2/\\alpha_1\\in\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt D]\n\\]\nhas norm one because \\(\\alpha_1^{-1}=-\\overline{\\alpha_1}\\). Write \\(\\gamma=a+b\\sqrt D\\). Then\n\\[\n2a=\\gamma+\\gamma^{-1}&gt;2,\n\\]\nso \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(\\gamma\\ge2+\\sqrt3\\). All \\(\\alpha\\le8N^2+1\\). Their fixed multiplicative spacing gives the bound uniformly in \\(D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Supplementary level-equation determinant estimate, original Section 12.3.] For coefficient vectors \\(A,B\\) in \\(R=\\mathbb Z[\\zeta]\\), let \\(T_0,S,C_2,C_3\\) be the coefficients of \\(1,\\zeta,\\zeta^2,\\zeta^3\\) in \\(A^2B\\).", "statement": "<p>[Supplementary level-equation determinant estimate, original Section 12.3.]</p><p>For coefficient vectors \\(A,B\\) in \\(R=\\mathbb Z[\\zeta]\\), let \\(T_0,S,C_2,C_3\\) be the coefficients of \\(1,\\zeta,\\zeta^2,\\zeta^3\\) in \\(A^2B\\). Their directions lie on\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_8=\\{C_2=C_3=0\\}\\subseteq\\mathbb P^3\\times\\mathbb P^3,\n\\]\nand counted integral representatives satisfy\n\\[\nT_0=n,\\qquad S=-1. \\tag{12.2}\n\\]\nWith hyperplane classes \\(H,J\\),\n\\[\n[\\mathcal V_8]=(2H+J)^2,\\qquad\n(H^4,H^3J,H^2J^2,HJ^3,J^4)_{\\mathcal V_8}=(0,1,4,4,0).\n\\]\nOn the split-coordinate torus,\n\\[\nA_i^2B_i=T_0+S\\zeta_i;\n\\]\nthe divisor \\(S=0\\) is the inverse-square graph, and \\(-S/T_0=1/n\\) at counted points.</p><p>Let \\(D\\subseteq\\mathcal V_8\\) be an integral direction variety of dimension \\(r\\ge1\\), meeting \\(S\\ne0\\). Write \\(R_D(K,L)\\) for restrictions of forms of bidegree \\((K,L)\\). Suppose a homogeneous degree-\\(r\\) polynomial \\(v_0(k,l)\\), with nonnegative mixed-degree coefficients, gives\n\\[\n\\dim R_D(Tk,Tl)\\ge\\frac{v_0(k,l)}{r!}T^r-O(T^{r-1}) \\tag{12.3}\n\\]\non fixed positive rational rays. Suppose polynomial-height integral lattice representatives satisfy\n\\[\n|A|\\ll N^h,\\qquad |B|\\ll N^g,\\qquad h,g\\ge0.\n\\]\nFor positive rational \\(k,l\\), let \\(a=\\min(k/2,l)\\), and assume \\(v_0(k,l)&gt;0\\). A nested selection of integral columns has average logarithmic size, divided by the common scale \\(T\\), at most\n\\[\nkh+lg-(2h+g)\\int_0^a\n\\frac{v_0(k-2j,l-j)}{v_0(k,l)}\\,dj+\\varepsilon. \\tag{12.4}\n\\]</p><p>If \\(D\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-defined and its integral model is smooth of relative dimension \\(r\\) at a projective residue class modulo a prime \\(q\\), then \\(q\\ge N^\\rho\\) forces a proper determinant cut whenever\n\\[\n\\rho&gt;\n\\frac{1+1/r}{v_0(k,l)^{1/r}}\n\\left\\{\nkh+lg-(2h+g)\\int_0^a\n\\frac{v_0(k-2j,l-j)}{v_0(k,l)}\\,dj\n\\right\\}. \\tag{12.5}\n\\]\nThe strict margin fixes mesh and determinant degree before \\(N\\). For curves with \\(a_1=H\\cdot D,b_1=J\\cdot D\\), the choice \\((k,l)=(2,1)\\) gives threshold\n\\[\n(2h+g)/(2a_1+b_1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The equations and intersection numbers are the \\(d=4,k=2\\) specialization of Proposition 4's complete intersection.</p><p>For bounded geometric degree, the lower Hilbert error is uniform on a fixed finite list of rays. After the corresponding projective embedding, linear Noether normalization gives a polynomial subring with field-extension degree equal to the projective degree \\(e\\). Monomials of degree at most \\(e-1\\) contain a field basis, since their spans increase strictly until filling the extension. Multiplication by powers of one normalization coordinate gives a graded injection of \\(e\\) polynomial-ring copies shifted by \\(e-1\\), with degree-\\(T\\) dimension\n\\[\ne\\binom{T-e+1+r}{r}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a rational mesh \\(0=j_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;j_s&lt;a\\). The spaces\n\\[\nS^{j_iT}R_D(T(k-2j_i),T(l-j_i))\n\\subseteq R_D(Tk,Tl)\n\\]\nare nested, and multiplication by \\(S\\) is injective. Choose decreasing integers\n\\[\nr_i=\\frac{v_0(k-2j_i,l-j_i)}{r!}T^r+O(T^{r-1})\n\\]\nbelow their dimensions, and put \\(P=r_0\\). Starting at the smallest space, extend independent generators at each stage. Each generator is an individual integral form \\(S^bM\\) of common bidegree. At counted points its value is \\((-1)^bM(A,B)\\), of size\n\\[\nO_T(N^{h(Tk-2b)+g(Tl-b)}).\n\\]\nAt least \\(r_i\\) columns have \\(b\\ge j_iT\\). Sum logarithmic sizes and refine the fixed mesh to obtain (12.4). No small coefficient bound for the rebased \\(S\\) is used.</p><p>At a smooth projective residue class, normalize by chart coordinates that are units modulo \\(q\\). Expansion in the \\(r\\) free differences gives valuation at least \\(s_r(P)\\), as in Lemma 2.2. Comparing this divisibility with (12.4) yields (12.5). For a curve, \\(v_0(k,l)=a_1k+b_1l\\); at \\((2,1)\\), the integral ratio is \\(1/2\\), giving the stated threshold. These are supplementary projective estimates; the density proof uses the affine estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Supplementary conditional analytic-relation estimate, original Section 12.4.] Fix \\(\\omega>0\\) and give \\(u_1,u_2,u_3,u_4\\) weights \\((\\omega,\\omega,\\omega,1)\\).", "statement": "<p>[Supplementary conditional analytic-relation estimate, original Section 12.4.]</p><p>Fix \\(\\omega&gt;0\\) and give \\(u_1,u_2,u_3,u_4\\) weights \\((\\omega,\\omega,\\omega,1)\\). Let \\(f_\\lambda\\) be holomorphic and uniformly bounded on one fixed complex polydisc about zero, with Taylor coefficients continuous at \\(\\lambda_0\\). Assume \\(f_{\\lambda_0}\\) is a nonzero germ of lowest weighted degree \\(D&gt;0\\).</p><p>Consider points with\n\\[\n|u_i|\\ll N^{-\\omega}\\ (1\\le i\\le3),\\quad\n|u_4|\\ll N^{-1},\\quad f_\\lambda(u)=0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\lambda\\) lies sufficiently near \\(\\lambda_0\\). Each evaluation matrix uses a single fixed \\(\\lambda\\) for all its points. All truncation levels and matrix sizes are fixed before \\(N\\) grows.</p><p>For an \\(\\ell\\times\\ell\\) matrix \\(G\\) of evaluations of analytic columns uniformly bounded on the same polydisc,\n\\[\n|\\det G|\n\\ll_\\ell\nN^{-\\frac34(6\\omega^3/D)^{1/3}\\ell^{4/3}+O(\\ell)}.\n\\]\nThe estimate concerns approximation rank, not exact rank of the analytic evaluation matrix. It is uniform on a compact parameter family of nonzero pulled-back germs with the same analytic bounds and coefficient continuity, by a finite cover. Normalizing original sections alone does not establish this nonvanishing hypothesis. A limiting germ with nonzero constant term instead has no such shrinking zeros near \\(\\lambda_0\\) for sufficiently large \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(E_Z\\) be the coefficient space of monomials of weight strictly less than \\(Z\\), with Euclidean coefficient norm. For fixed \\(Z&gt;D\\), truncated multiplication gives\n\\[\nM_\\lambda:E_{Z-D}\\to E_Z,\\qquad\np\\mapsto[pf_\\lambda]_{&lt;Z}.\n\\]\nIt is injective at \\(\\lambda_0\\): lowest weighted parts of nonzero polynomials have nonzero product, and the lowest weight of \\(pf_{\\lambda_0}\\) is below \\(Z\\). A nonzero maximal minor persists near \\(\\lambda_0\\), giving a uniformly bounded left inverse on its image \\(W_\\lambda\\).</p><p>At the chosen zeros, every truncated multiple \\([u^\\alpha f_\\lambda]_{&lt;Z}\\) is \\(O_Z(N^{-Z})\\). Expand to ordinary order \\(K\\) with \\(\\min(\\omega,1)(K+1)\\ge Z\\); omitted finite terms and the Taylor tail have this bound, and the full multiple vanishes. The bounded left inverse gives the same evaluation bound for each unit-norm member of \\(W_\\lambda\\).</p><p>Truncate each analytic column to \\(E_Z\\) and project onto \\(W_\\lambda^\\perp\\). The evaluation matrix changes entrywise by \\(O_Z(N^{-Z})\\), while the projected matrix has rank at most\n\\[\nq_Z=\\dim E_Z-\\dim E_{Z-D}\n=\\frac{D}{6\\omega^3}Z^3+O_{D,\\omega}(Z^2+1).\n\\]\nChoose a lowest monomial \\(u^\\gamma\\) of the limiting relation, of weight \\(D\\). The difference counts monomials below \\(Z\\) not divisible by \\(u^\\gamma\\). Their exponents lie in strips \\(\\alpha_i&lt;\\gamma_i\\); individual strips give the leading coefficient \\(D/(6\\omega^3)\\), and pairwise overlaps contribute \\(O(Z^2+1)\\).</p><p>If \\(q_Z&lt;\\ell\\), singular values satisfy\n\\[\n\\sigma_{q_Z+1}(G)\\ll_{\\ell,Z}N^{-Z}.\n\\]\nFor the \\(j\\)-th singular value choose\n\\[\nZ=(6\\omega^3j/D)^{1/3}-O(1).\n\\]\nSumming these finitely many levels and multiplying singular-value bounds gives the determinant estimate. Small indices with level at most \\(D\\) use the uniform \\(O(1)\\) matrix bound and enter \\(O(\\ell)\\).</p><p>Only finitely many truncation levels are used, so no convergence rate for \\(\\lambda\\to\\lambda_0\\) is needed. A finite cover gives compact-family uniformity; if orders vary, use the largest order among its finitely many centers.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Supplementary box-incidence estimate, original Section 12.4.] Fix \\(C\\ge1\\) and \\(M>T\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>[Supplementary box-incidence estimate, original Section 12.4.]</p><p>Fix \\(C\\ge1\\) and \\(M&gt;T\\ge1\\). For grid bases \\(c=(c_1,c_2,c_3)\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\), \\(|c_i|\\le CM\\), call a nonzero \\(v=(v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3)\\in\\mathbb Z^4\\) a witness if\n\\[\n|v_0|\\le T,\\qquad |Mv_i-c_iv_0|\\le T\\quad(1\\le i\\le3).\n\\]\nThen \\(v_0\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\|v\\|_\\infty\\asymp_C|v_0|,\\qquad \\|v\\|_\\infty\\ll_CT,\n\\]\nand a fixed \\(v\\) witnesses at most\n\\[\nO_C\\left((T/\\|v\\|_\\infty)^3\\right)\n\\]\ngrid boxes. These are original coordinate vectors, distinct from the residual vectors used in Proposition 5.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(v_0=0\\), then \\(M&gt;T\\) forces every \\(v_i=0\\), a contradiction. Moreover,\n\\[\n|v_i|\\le C|v_0|+T/M\\le(C+1)|v_0|.\n\\]\nThus the norm comparisons hold. For fixed \\(v\\), each \\(c_i\\) lies in an interval of length \\(2T/|v_0|\\), giving \\(O_C(T/|v_0|)\\) integer possibilities because \\(|v_0|\\le T\\). Multiply the three bounds.</p><p>In the density argument, Proposition 5 uses a single prime approximation grid to count boxes; Proposition 2 handles adaptive auxiliary primes separately inside each transformed affine box.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 31, "cite"], [8, 35, "cite"], [8, 36, "cite"], [8, 43, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 41, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [12, 19, "cite"], [12, 21, "cite"], [12, 36, "cite"], [13, 4, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 21, "cite"], [15, 22, "cite"], [15, 31, "cite"], [15, 36, "cite"], [15, 41, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [16, 24, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [19, 36, "cite"], [19, 43, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [22, 25, "cite"], [22, 36, "cite"], [23, 22, "cite"], [24, 22, "cite"], [25, 36, "cite"], [26, 29, "cite"], [26, 36, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [29, 36, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [30, 35, "cite"], [31, 34, "cite"], [31, 35, "cite"], [31, 36, "cite"], [32, 4, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 0, "cite"], [34, 4, "cite"], [34, 37, "cite"], [35, 34, "cite"], [36, 4, "cite"], [36, 34, "cite"], [37, 2, "cite"], [39, 38, "cite"], [40, 38, "cite"]]});
